support that you've given

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:53.700
to Jim and all of us over the last year.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.150
And good luck as you refocus back at base.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.383
- Escape.

00:01:58.383 --> 00:01:59.273
- Escape. Exactly.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.950
Do we have any declarations of interest

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:07.473
over and above those that
are already published?

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:12.450
Nope. Okay. Thank you very much.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:14.850
And I would just like to share

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:17.040
so we have a wonderful gentleman, Bren,

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:18.000
just to embarrass him,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.520
who turns up to all of
our meetings in public.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.620
He's sitting here in the audience today,

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.530
who's been doing a great
job of giving me feedback

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:28.470
about these meetings, which
I very much appreciate.

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:31.893
And a few consistent themes that come up.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:38.030
One is that we do have a real
tendency to use acronyms.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.410
We are not good at that. We know that.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.570
I think we are all
becoming conscious of it,

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:45.540
but we need to be super conscious of it.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:48.690
So I will try hard and I
would ask all of my colleagues

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:52.050
to try hard to use
straightforward language

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:55.860
rather than continually shortening things.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.200
I think that would feel very
positive and very helpful

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.513
to members of the public
who are listening in.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:06.830
We do need to recognise
that everybody in this room

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:10.530
and one and a half million
colleagues across the NHS

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:14.640
work in difficult circumstances
for quite a lot of the time.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.640
We are asking people to
provide fantastic quality care

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:23.580
at a time when sometimes it
can feel difficult to do that.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:25.140
We are all working on that.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:26.610
We'll have a whole set
of conversations today

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:28.470
about the work that's taking place,

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:31.470
but we do need to
recognise, and I know that

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.690
all of our colleagues do that

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:35.580
people are putting in a lot of hard work

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.190
and we really thank them for that.

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:39.933
I think that's really important.

00:03:40.822 --> 00:03:43.950
At the same time, we want to be looking at

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:46.080
what are we doing in a constructive way,

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.117
where are there
opportunities for improvement

00:03:48.117 --> 00:03:50.760
and we will do that as we go
through the material today,

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:52.770
both looking at where there
are those opportunities,

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:56.490
but also what we are doing
to positively change things

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:59.430
and how we are going to know
that things are getting better.

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:03.510
So that a whole theme
of the meeting today.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:05.250
Lots of other things that Bren is sharing.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:06.180
I won't go through all of that,

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:07.200
otherwise we'd be here for a while,

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.307
but thank you Bren for that,

00:04:08.307 --> 00:04:11.700
and I look forward to more
feedback from today's meeting.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:12.903
It is appreciated.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:16.200
Onto the substantive items

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:19.140
the first one is the minutes
from the last meeting

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:20.490
on the 5th of February.

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:23.253
Does anybody have any comments on those?

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:29.340
Okay, so we'll take those
as read and approved.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:31.200
The next item is updates

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.270
from the various board subcommittees.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.730
So I'd like to ask colleagues

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.130
particularly the committee
chairs and exec colleagues

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:40.083
to share updates from those.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:43.560
As a move from last time
we have asked for those

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:44.820
to be a bit more comprehensive,

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:47.490
to share a bit more of the
meat of the conversation,

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:49.410
again, for full transparency.

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.450
So I'm going to start
with the people committee

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:53.700
and with Louise.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:54.533
- Thank you, chair.

00:04:54.533 --> 00:04:58.440
So, yes, more detail than previously.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:00.090
Only a couple of things I'd
like to pull out really.

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:03.150
And one is the oversight we've got

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:05.070
on the voluntary redundancy programme,

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:10.070
which obviously I'm getting a
weekly update on that and Jim

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:12.480
and colleagues are working hard to ensure

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:16.080
that staff are cared
for through the process

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.910
and actually a lot of staff
in NHS England are leaving

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:24.120
in fact this week, and a
number are leaving in June

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:25.950
and obviously, I'm sure Jim and everybody

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:28.800
wants to thank those staff
for their fantastic service

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:31.353
during their time at NHS England.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.650
One of the issues that we talked about,

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:36.840
and I think Mark is also raising this

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:41.840
in his committee paper is
about the need to retain

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:44.550
the right capability,
particularly in the digital data

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:49.410
and technology team and perhaps Mark

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:51.495
might want to raise that as well

00:05:51.495 --> 00:05:52.800
as part of his committee update.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.036
And at the next meeting,

00:05:55.036 --> 00:05:57.270
we're going to be looking
at workforce planning

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:59.160
against the business plan for NHS England

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:01.860
and trying to have oversight of you know,

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:05.250
having the right stuff in the right jobs

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:07.350
for the business plan for the coming year.

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:12.060
Sarah-Jane Marsh is now overseeing

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:14.610
the Improving NHS England Together

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:17.520
Organisational Culture Programme,

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:19.410
and we had an update from her.

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:22.380
We looked at statutory mandatory training,

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:25.050
some really interesting analysis

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.740
of statutory mandatory
training sort of mandated

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:31.860
from the centre that
isn't actually showing

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:36.860
a huge amount of evidence
that it is improving outcomes

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:39.720
for people so we're doing
lots more work on that

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:41.760
to see what statutory mandatory training

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:44.730
is absolutely necessary

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:48.360
and we are increasing the
frequency of our meetings

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.630
of the People Committee
and we've got one coming up

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:51.633
in a few weeks time.

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:53.767
- That's great. Thank you.

00:06:53.767 --> 00:06:55.983
Does anyone have any questions for Louise?

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:01.852
- Just to add on, I really
accept the challenges.

00:07:01.852 --> 00:07:03.120
I take on some new responsibilities

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:04.410
around the talent management

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:06.090
and the identification of people,

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:08.280
the ones that really do the
work in this organisation

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:11.180
that sit at the tiers, the
next couple of tiers from here.

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:15.210
We need to be providing
really clear opportunities

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:18.030
for those people to be
both aligned to our purpose

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:19.680
and our priorities and our values,

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:21.630
but also to keep their energy really high

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:23.160
and what we know will
be a challenging time

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:24.390
that you alluded to, Penny.

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:26.910
So that's going to be
one of the key priorities

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:29.430
over the next few weeks.

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:31.050
That will be our success or not won't it

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.850
over the next year really is our ability

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:35.790
to be able to keep those
people energised and aligned.

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:39.303
- Yeah, agree and thank you for
taking on that work. Robert?

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:41.603
- Two quick questions.

00:07:41.603 --> 00:07:44.343
One is under talent and appointments.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.000
And we talked in the
past about some training

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.100
for non-executive directors on boards,

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:52.260
which I think could be very useful

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:53.760
to maximise their effectiveness.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:56.070
I'm not sure if that's happening.

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:58.200
The other is I'm not sure
if there's the right place

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:01.050
to raise this, but we agreed sometime ago

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:04.830
to appoint a director of
experience as an executive

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:08.523
on this board reporting
I think directly to Jim.

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:10.650
It seems to have been slow,

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:14.343
just an update on how that
appointment is progressing.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:19.440
- Thank you, Robert, for raising that.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:22.950
I've been raising it possibly
twice weekly with Duncan

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:24.690
and others, and I'm sure
Duncan will have an update

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:27.510
on that in terms of the
director of patient experience

00:08:27.510 --> 00:08:31.770
because that really does
need to be you know,

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:33.870
go out there for recruitment.

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:37.620
I know that the job
description has been completed.

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:39.770
And then the point of
our non-exec director

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:44.790
and chair development in the
system, there's a big piece

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:47.100
of work going on that, and of
course there is this setting

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:50.430
up of the NHS Leadership College,

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:54.289
which is progressing so
that is certainly a priority

00:08:54.289 --> 00:08:55.830
that the committee will be looking at.

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:57.660
I don't know, Duncan,
if you want to address

00:08:57.660 --> 00:08:58.980
the former point.

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:02.220
- The new patient experience
director role should be out

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:04.770
to advert I'm promised by
the end of next week, so.

00:09:07.564 --> 00:09:08.670
when it when it comes out.

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:10.620
- Good. We look forward to that. Great.

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:14.613
Okay. Let's move on to strategy, David?

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:19.110
- Thank you, so the focus
of the committee continues

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:20.580
to be on four things.

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:23.610
So first of all, making
sure we really understand

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:26.010
what is going on across both NHS England

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:29.820
and the department in terms
of strategy and policy work,

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:32.220
and making sure that each of the projects

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:36.630
has a steering committee or equivalent

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:39.030
that is overseeing the work in advance

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:41.463
of it coming to the board for decision.

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:45.330
And so for the strategy committee itself,

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:50.130
we identified 14 projects
which we will be overseeing

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:55.130
and in the appendix, there's
a list of those 14 topics.

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:57.880
So that's the first thing, then secondly,

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:01.620
we are in the process of
agreeing a standardised model

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:05.250
for how we do this
strategy and policy work.

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:08.160
That's partly about making
sure we have consistent quality

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:10.500
in the work, partly about making sure

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:14.100
that all the interdependencies
and there are a huge number

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:19.100
of interdependencies, are being
addressed and coordinated,

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:22.980
make sure we appropriately
prioritising all this work

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:25.413
and making effective use of our resources.

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:30.720
We discussed at our last
meeting a draft paper

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:35.250
on this proposed model and
this has now been subject

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:39.240
to more detailed review
amongst the executive team.

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:40.073
So the second thing,

00:10:40.073 --> 00:10:43.290
third thing is looking at the development

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:47.250
of an overarching model that
pulls all of this together

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:48.960
so that we have a real understanding

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:53.670
of what it is we're aiming
for with the 10 year plan

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:55.653
in a more quantitative sense,

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:59.100
ultimately of course it's all
about the impact on patients,

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:03.360
but then how that translates
into financial impact,

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:08.360
staff numbers and all the
other things that go into this.

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:12.870
And again, we're in the
process of developing this

00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:17.490
and in fact we'll be discussing
it a bit later today.

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:19.650
And then finally we've been reviewing

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:24.650
some of those 14 projects
that we have chosen

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:27.390
for the strategy committee
to look at ourselves

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:32.370
and those that we looked
at our last meeting

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:33.843
also listed in the paper.

00:11:34.950 --> 00:11:35.790
- Okay. Great. Thank you.

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:38.215
I think probably also worthwhile
saying we have also managed

00:11:38.215 --> 00:11:39.048
to get a few things out the door,

00:11:39.048 --> 00:11:41.700
like the women's health
strategy I think was published

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:44.444
just as one example of things.

00:11:44.444 --> 00:11:45.314
- [Duncan] It's pending publications.

00:11:45.314 --> 00:11:47.560
- Oh, sorry, pending publication.

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:48.393
- [Duncan] In the next couple of weeks.

00:11:48.393 --> 00:11:50.538
- Okay. Yeah. I'm hearing...

00:11:50.538 --> 00:11:52.110
Because I saw Secretary of
State's speech yesterday

00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:53.790
refer to it in two weeks time,

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:55.350
but then I was at a meeting yesterday

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:57.870
when someone told me that
it had been published.

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:02.100
- No, it's just due to be.
- Okay. All right. Okay.

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:03.813
Any questions for David?

00:12:06.450 --> 00:12:09.360
Okay, great. Jolly good.

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:14.010
The next one is a new
subcommittee of the board

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:17.250
which I am chairing, so I
have to talk to this myself.

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:19.260
So this is a new group we've set up

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:23.100
called the Research
Innovation and Growth Board,

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:25.680
and the purpose of that was to ensure

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:27.300
that we had closer alignment

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:29.850
to the life sciences sector delivery plan,

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:33.990
which is a key piece of work
that was published last year

00:12:33.990 --> 00:12:36.810
and to ensure that we are
delivering against our side

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:39.840
of the objectives within that.

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:43.800
But also to very much
embrace the wider commercial

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:47.350
and innovation agenda, which
Fiona is currently leading on

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:52.110
to ensure that we are giving
sufficient focus to research,

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:54.540
but also that we are supporting

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:56.130
the health innovation networks

00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:58.680
and a much wider group of organisations

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:00.390
and colleagues focused on innovation

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:01.980
out there in the system.

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:03.660
So it's a fairly broad agenda,

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:06.630
but also feels like a crucial agenda

00:13:06.630 --> 00:13:08.610
for a number of elements
of the 10 year plan

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:12.213
as well as the wider areas
that I've referred to.

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:16.020
The first meeting focused
on terms of reference,

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:18.243
membership, the title of the group,

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:22.260
looked at how we are
clearer on accountabilities

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:25.620
from that life sciences sector plan,

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:28.620
but also reflected on things
like a interesting meeting

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:32.280
that was held with a number
of med tech companies

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:35.880
who at times find it
quite difficult to ensure

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:40.200
that the latest innovations
and products are safely

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:43.020
and sensibly and in a
financially robust way

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:44.520
adopted into the NHS.

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:46.560
So we talked about the links between that

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:48.030
and the modern service frameworks,

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:50.850
which we will be coming
on to talk about shortly.

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:54.750
I think overall sense was
it was a good meeting.

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:56.880
We are going to have regular updates

00:13:56.880 --> 00:13:58.950
from the NHS Genomics Programme,

00:13:58.950 --> 00:14:01.290
which is a key bit, again, of innovation

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:04.560
and forward looking, and
we'll start to put together

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:06.390
a list of topics that we'll be discussing

00:14:06.390 --> 00:14:07.923
over the forthcoming months.

00:14:09.630 --> 00:14:12.060
Any questions, comments?

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:12.893
- Just a comment.

00:14:12.893 --> 00:14:13.887
I mean, I think it was a very good meeting

00:14:13.887 --> 00:14:17.820
and it's great that it's taken shape.

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:21.690
I think it'll be important
to define some metrics

00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:22.920
of what we should be measuring

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:24.990
and I've arranged to
meet with Lucy Chappell,

00:14:24.990 --> 00:14:29.220
the Director General to
get my thoughts in shape,

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:31.214
which I'll then share with you.

00:14:31.214 --> 00:14:33.330
- Okay, great. Thank you.

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:36.873
Okay, good. Let's go on
to digital and data then.

00:14:38.910 --> 00:14:43.170
- So good meetings,
meeting broadly separated

00:14:43.170 --> 00:14:44.003
into two areas.

00:14:44.003 --> 00:14:48.280
One, cyber and resilience,
and the second is delivering

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:51.660
digital change across the system.

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:54.480
In terms of cyber and resilience,

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:58.710
pushing very hard on
really driving through

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:00.810
the resilience programme
that started 18 months ago

00:15:00.810 --> 00:15:02.820
and making sure that the
critical national infrastructure

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:05.220
is where it should be and the
teams are making good progress

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:08.940
and this is sort of long diligent work.

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:12.150
And then also making sure we can respond

00:15:12.150 --> 00:15:14.530
to any cyber incident if it ever happened

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:19.627
and progress is now being
made on a national test

00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:24.630
of what we would do in that situation

00:15:24.630 --> 00:15:26.460
and making sure we can
actually respond effectively.

00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:29.670
And there's been some good
progress over the last week.

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:32.340
To Louise's point one of the concerns

00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:37.340
is we have lost people in this
area due to the VR programme

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:40.590
and the team are going back through

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:42.245
just to make sure we
have the right resources

00:15:42.245 --> 00:15:43.245
in the right places.

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:47.550
And then on delivering digital change,

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:50.220
really good progress by the team

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:54.210
on what we would actually
do in sort of turning

00:15:54.210 --> 00:15:55.770
the aspiration of the 10 year plan

00:15:55.770 --> 00:15:59.010
into actual delivery plans and
things we would accelerate.

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:01.230
I think there's a few
things that come out of it.

00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:03.450
One, the resourcing issue pops back

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:06.377
that we're going to have
to make some prioritisation

00:16:06.377 --> 00:16:08.913
decisions because we don't
have the headcount we did.

00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:14.760
Two, we need...

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:18.990
As you move from sort of
technology that sits nationally

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:21.870
to experience that cuts through the system

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:24.780
on a patient basis, we need
to find much closer alignment

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:27.090
with the operations and the business

00:16:27.090 --> 00:16:30.990
and having people who are sort
of in a traditional business,

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:34.410
the chief product owner for
urgent and emergency care.

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:37.350
And I think Paul's doing
some great work on that

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:39.790
and we can actually see real progress

00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:42.180
over the last three months

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:44.400
in terms of how people are addressing it.

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:48.480
And then finally we've
finished off the work

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:51.840
finding the permanent leadership
of the area, which is good,

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:54.603
which hopefully will be
resolved in the next few weeks.

00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:57.903
- Okay, great. Any questions?

00:16:59.700 --> 00:17:02.190
Nope, okay. So then let's move on.

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:05.580
So the next three committees
have all met recently,

00:17:05.580 --> 00:17:07.530
so that's why we don't have a write up,

00:17:07.530 --> 00:17:09.750
but we're going to have a verbal update.

00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:12.723
So audit and risk committee, Jeremy?

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:15.660
- Yeah, so we had our second joint meeting

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:17.820
with the department.

00:17:17.820 --> 00:17:19.680
One of the key risks that we're looking at

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:22.150
is the whole transition risk

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:26.010
and focusing on the date in particular.

00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:28.320
There's a risk that the legislation...

00:17:28.320 --> 00:17:30.630
The legislation's in process at the moment

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:33.570
and there's no plan to move the date,

00:17:33.570 --> 00:17:35.340
but there's a risk the date may move.

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:38.280
And so we need a contingency
plan around that date

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:41.430
being delayed and if the
date was to be delayed

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:44.280
what would be the next best date?

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:47.190
So that contingency
plan's being worked up.

00:17:47.190 --> 00:17:49.890
There's a similar but asymmetric risk

00:17:49.890 --> 00:17:53.040
that the legislation does
take place on the date,

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:55.890
but all the responsibilities
or all of the issues

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:58.560
relating to transition
haven't been fully resolved.

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:00.300
We need to identify what those risks are

00:18:00.300 --> 00:18:02.337
and what the mitigation
plans and contingency plans

00:18:02.337 --> 00:18:03.330
are around that.

00:18:03.330 --> 00:18:05.970
So the team working on the transition

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:07.740
and working on those contingency plans,

00:18:07.740 --> 00:18:11.460
and we're going to pick that
up at our next joint meeting.

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:13.800
The joint meeting also
looked at the respective

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:17.730
risk registers of the
department and NHS England.

00:18:17.730 --> 00:18:21.870
They're quite different
but not incompatible

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:24.180
The department tends to
be looking out more at

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:27.150
national risks around resilience.

00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:30.660
The NHS England is more
looking at how the NHS delivers

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:35.340
its issues, but there weren't
anything that was of concern.

00:18:35.340 --> 00:18:37.710
We'll continue to run those separately

00:18:37.710 --> 00:18:39.453
but review them together.

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:43.383
Within the NHS England audit committee,

00:18:44.610 --> 00:18:49.500
our key issue, we talked about
the cyber resilience excise

00:18:49.500 --> 00:18:51.720
and it's great to hear that's picking up.

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:55.185
We appreciate as we
move towards transition,

00:18:55.185 --> 00:18:57.360
there may be a more challenged
control environment,

00:18:57.360 --> 00:19:00.000
particularly in the VR situation as well

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.400
and what we felt that the commission-

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:04.770
- We keep saying VR equals
voluntary redundancy.

00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:05.603
- Sorry, voluntarily redundancy

00:19:05.603 --> 00:19:08.193
I told myself I should not
use any TLAs, so apologies.

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:15.420
To some extent for
Elizabeth and Jim to judge

00:19:15.420 --> 00:19:17.340
what the priorities are around control

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:19.860
and let's not have a situation

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:21.390
where we're trying to cover everything

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:22.860
and not deliver on the key issues.

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:26.400
So I think that's probably an
issue for the area as a whole.

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:29.340
And then finally, I'll
just make this plug.

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:31.890
An issue of mine for the last five years

00:19:31.890 --> 00:19:34.020
has been trying to look at
the accounting situation

00:19:34.020 --> 00:19:34.950
and the audit situation.

00:19:34.950 --> 00:19:38.790
We spent about 60 million
pounds auditing the trust.

00:19:38.790 --> 00:19:41.520
Is there an easier way of
delivering the accountability

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:44.760
and the comfort that we need without doing

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:45.870
the full scale audits?

00:19:45.870 --> 00:19:48.000
And there's a project reset at the moment

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:50.520
that's looking at all
that and I would hope

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:52.710
there's an opportunity
to save some money there,

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:56.280
but still deliver the
accountabilities we'd like to deliver.

00:19:56.280 --> 00:19:59.553
- Great. Thank you. Any
comments, questions?

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:02.850
Okay, let's move on to finance

00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:04.380
and performance committee, Andrew?

00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:05.213
- Thanks, Penny.

00:20:05.213 --> 00:20:08.460
So we spent some time kicking the tyres

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:11.667
on month 10 where we've got to on month 10

00:20:11.667 --> 00:20:12.840
and the planning round.

00:20:12.840 --> 00:20:17.550
So at month 10, it looks
like the financial reset

00:20:17.550 --> 00:20:19.560
is holding, which is fabulous news

00:20:19.560 --> 00:20:21.480
and well done to everyone.

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:22.560
A great bit of leadership

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:25.023
on behalf of colleagues to my right.

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:30.870
So 67% of systems and 90% of providers

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:33.540
are saying they're going to
hit their target this year,

00:20:33.540 --> 00:20:35.880
which is, you know, a far better position

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:39.900
than it was this time last
year which is great to see.

00:20:39.900 --> 00:20:44.790
Eight billion has been
delivered in efficiencies

00:20:44.790 --> 00:20:49.770
and boards are gripping
the money to a degree

00:20:49.770 --> 00:20:52.473
that we've not seen or experienced before.

00:20:53.730 --> 00:20:58.620
So it's looking really, really
good and we should land this.

00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:00.630
And lots of boards have put in

00:21:00.630 --> 00:21:02.070
a tremendous amount of effort.

00:21:02.070 --> 00:21:05.370
It's been a big priority in most settings

00:21:05.370 --> 00:21:08.580
around financial reset
and it's really good

00:21:08.580 --> 00:21:12.360
that some historically challenged
systems have come good.

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:16.530
So if you take colleagues
in Lancs and South Cumbria,

00:21:16.530 --> 00:21:20.040
Greater Manchester,
Shropshire and Staffordshire,

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:22.590
Norfolk and Waveney,
and last but not least,

00:21:22.590 --> 00:21:25.920
Shrewsbury and Telford have
moved into a really good place

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:27.513
and delivered on the numbers.

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:31.620
So they've been troubled
systems for several years

00:21:31.620 --> 00:21:34.650
and it's really, really
positive that you know,

00:21:34.650 --> 00:21:37.380
things have come good in those settings.

00:21:37.380 --> 00:21:40.980
On the planning round things
are looking pretty good,

00:21:40.980 --> 00:21:44.610
so we've got various iterations
that we're going through.

00:21:44.610 --> 00:21:49.610
So 35 out of 36 systems are saying

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:51.460
they will hit their plan limit

00:21:52.320 --> 00:21:57.320
going forward into next
year, and 133 out of 204

00:21:58.050 --> 00:22:00.240
providers are saying they'll do the same.

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:01.980
So again, we're in a stronger position.

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:04.830
So if you look at where we
were this time last year,

00:22:04.830 --> 00:22:09.830
we had a gap of about 4.4
billion and it's now 1.45

00:22:10.407 --> 00:22:12.120
and there's a bit more to do.

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:16.950
So if you look at where we
are on delivering performance

00:22:16.950 --> 00:22:20.610
for next year, we're almost, almost there

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:22.980
with systems saying that they will hit

00:22:22.980 --> 00:22:25.410
the respective targets that we've got

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:28.320
particularly around the
constitutional standards.

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:31.840
Efficiency is still
really tough out there,

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:34.080
and I think in some patches,

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:36.150
providers are really, really struggling,

00:22:36.150 --> 00:22:38.610
but overall, it was a great meeting

00:22:38.610 --> 00:22:41.163
and some really, really positive results.

00:22:42.180 --> 00:22:45.870
On performance, the big
thing that stood out

00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:48.600
for the committee is where we've got to,

00:22:48.600 --> 00:22:52.350
which is a really bad
place on life expectancy.

00:22:52.350 --> 00:22:56.890
So we are two years behind
where we were pre-pandemic

00:22:57.840 --> 00:23:01.620
which isn't a great place and
it's not all down to the NHS,

00:23:01.620 --> 00:23:06.120
but I think we need to be a
lot clearer as a board around

00:23:06.120 --> 00:23:08.970
what is it that's within the 10 year plan

00:23:08.970 --> 00:23:11.940
that's going to give us the
shift back to where we were,

00:23:11.940 --> 00:23:15.420
and I think we're quite
uncited at the moment

00:23:15.420 --> 00:23:17.973
around what those things are.

00:23:18.810 --> 00:23:20.850
So I think there's a bit of
work that we've got to do

00:23:20.850 --> 00:23:23.250
as a board just to get a fix on

00:23:23.250 --> 00:23:25.216
what are the big ticket items

00:23:25.216 --> 00:23:26.233
that are going to make the shift

00:23:26.233 --> 00:23:29.100
because two years and, you know,
I'm getting involved in it,

00:23:29.100 --> 00:23:34.100
I'm 70 you know, it means an
awful lot as you get older.

00:23:34.620 --> 00:23:37.500
And, you know, all of us
want to make it into our 90s

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:41.103
potentially if we can, and
that's our job in essence.

00:23:42.390 --> 00:23:46.530
So the other bit on performance
was around maternity.

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:49.060
So we've had several
conversations as a board

00:23:50.910 --> 00:23:53.700
around where we've got to on maternity,

00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:54.799
which isn't a great place in some

00:23:54.799 --> 00:23:57.510
patches and where we need to be.

00:23:57.510 --> 00:24:01.020
And I think we need a set of
metrics that we can monitor

00:24:01.020 --> 00:24:02.430
as part of the performance pack.

00:24:02.430 --> 00:24:05.430
So that's something hopefully
that Duncan will help us

00:24:05.430 --> 00:24:08.790
focus on going forward.

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:10.680
The last bit was on workforce.

00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:14.340
So Danny presented the workforce plan,

00:24:14.340 --> 00:24:16.680
so we've seen some big shifts,

00:24:16.680 --> 00:24:20.640
43% reduction in agency
staff across the service,

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:23.940
which is fantastic, better
quality care as a result,

00:24:23.940 --> 00:24:28.940
and it's cheaper and 7%
reduction in bank staff.

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:32.580
So that's one billion, that's one billion

00:24:32.580 --> 00:24:37.023
of the eight billion that
we've delivered on efficiency.

00:24:38.220 --> 00:24:41.700
And that has to have a
kind of continued focus

00:24:41.700 --> 00:24:42.900
going forward.

00:24:42.900 --> 00:24:45.780
Sickness is a worry and that's a focus

00:24:45.780 --> 00:24:47.880
I think we'll ask Danny to come back

00:24:47.880 --> 00:24:51.150
to the committee on because
it's going the wrong way.

00:24:51.150 --> 00:24:51.983
It's at 5.6.

00:24:53.310 --> 00:24:56.190
So we're almost hitting
kind of the pandemic levels

00:24:56.190 --> 00:24:58.650
of sickness that we had in the service.

00:24:58.650 --> 00:25:01.830
And it's a big issue, and I don't believe

00:25:01.830 --> 00:25:05.610
it's you know one that we can't resolve.

00:25:05.610 --> 00:25:09.060
I think with some really, really
good first line management

00:25:09.060 --> 00:25:11.100
support to get on top of this

00:25:11.100 --> 00:25:14.940
around supporting individuals
to come back to work.

00:25:14.940 --> 00:25:19.860
We can see that number reduced
back to our target is 4.1%

00:25:20.790 --> 00:25:24.420
and, you know, 1% of the
pay bill is a lot of money.

00:25:24.420 --> 00:25:26.850
There's a lot of money
there that will help us

00:25:26.850 --> 00:25:29.133
with our challenge on efficiency.

00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:32.190
And again, going forward,

00:25:32.190 --> 00:25:33.840
if you look at the performance pack,

00:25:33.840 --> 00:25:35.640
we've got relatively little data

00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:37.470
on where we are with people.

00:25:37.470 --> 00:25:41.730
So stuff like turnover, where
we are on the workforce plan,

00:25:41.730 --> 00:25:44.340
training numbers, productivity,

00:25:44.340 --> 00:25:46.920
just tracking where we are per, you know,

00:25:46.920 --> 00:25:50.040
consultant or activity
levels per consultant

00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:52.410
or per individual members of staff.

00:25:52.410 --> 00:25:54.300
I think that's something
we need to focus on.

00:25:54.300 --> 00:25:56.700
So they're the things that we covered

00:25:56.700 --> 00:26:01.700
and a big thank you to Jim
and Elizabeth on the money,

00:26:02.010 --> 00:26:05.670
because I think they have
galvanised the service into,

00:26:05.670 --> 00:26:07.290
you know, getting boards to deliver,

00:26:07.290 --> 00:26:10.740
getting boards to focus on this
as the number one priority,

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:14.550
and it just gives us a
bit more headroom as we go

00:26:14.550 --> 00:26:18.060
and credibility as we go
into next financial year.

00:26:18.060 --> 00:26:20.430
So thank you guys. Really well done.

00:26:20.430 --> 00:26:23.220
- Okay. Thank you, Andrew, for that.

00:26:23.220 --> 00:26:25.080
I think we'll get into
this a little bit more

00:26:25.080 --> 00:26:28.500
as we get into the performance report.

00:26:28.500 --> 00:26:30.160
Big thank you to Elizabeth

00:26:31.265 --> 00:26:33.292
and to Jim for our leadership here,

00:26:33.292 --> 00:26:34.408
do you want to come in on?

00:26:34.408 --> 00:26:36.717
- Yeah, just to highlight to members

00:26:36.717 --> 00:26:39.450
that it was a really good discussion,

00:26:39.450 --> 00:26:42.000
and it's great actually to have the Ned,

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:45.870
the quality of the Ned
conversation in the subcommittees.

00:26:45.870 --> 00:26:49.260
The numbers that Andrew quoted on planning

00:26:49.260 --> 00:26:51.660
were as of the February submission

00:26:51.660 --> 00:26:53.910
and since then we've
had a March submission

00:26:53.910 --> 00:26:58.290
and we've got a subsequent
submission today, so.

00:26:58.290 --> 00:27:00.060
I'm pleased to say to the board members,

00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:02.580
I know we'll cover this
in some detail later

00:27:02.580 --> 00:27:04.980
but the position even that Andrew quoted,

00:27:04.980 --> 00:27:07.680
which was a stark improvement
from this time last year

00:27:07.680 --> 00:27:09.483
has improved significantly again.

00:27:10.980 --> 00:27:15.720
- Great, we'll come onto
quality in a minute,

00:27:15.720 --> 00:27:20.250
but just one observation
and I'm looking at Katie

00:27:20.250 --> 00:27:21.933
who helps me with the agenda.

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:24.750
I think just picking
up on what Andrew said,

00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:26.379
it would be helpful.

00:27:26.379 --> 00:27:30.815
I think if we started these
updates with performance,

00:27:30.815 --> 00:27:34.710
you know, where are we now
and then go into strategy,

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:38.610
so just on those two, is
our strategy helping us

00:27:38.610 --> 00:27:39.810
to address our performance?

00:27:39.810 --> 00:27:41.010
I think that should be our sequence.

00:27:41.010 --> 00:27:44.850
So for future, just a note on
that, if we start with that.

00:27:44.850 --> 00:27:47.910
So quality, we had a intensive
quality day yesterday

00:27:47.910 --> 00:27:51.780
with both quality committee
and the national quality board.

00:27:51.780 --> 00:27:54.420
Robert, do you want to
share what we discussed?

00:27:54.420 --> 00:27:56.703
- Yes, it was. It was a quality day.

00:27:58.143 --> 00:27:59.460
And it was interesting

00:27:59.460 --> 00:28:02.520
because it was the final
meeting of the quality committee

00:28:02.520 --> 00:28:05.640
as a freestanding entity, and
we've discussed this before,

00:28:05.640 --> 00:28:08.580
the decision we took to avoid duplication

00:28:08.580 --> 00:28:13.020
and create greater efficiency
was to integrate these two.

00:28:13.020 --> 00:28:16.020
For governance purposes,
while NHS England exists,

00:28:16.020 --> 00:28:18.660
there needs to be a quality
committee of this board.

00:28:18.660 --> 00:28:20.400
And so what we're going to do

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:21.960
is have the National Quality Board

00:28:21.960 --> 00:28:25.200
and then as a kind of part
two the quality committee

00:28:25.200 --> 00:28:27.570
and we're shaping exactly what that agenda

00:28:27.570 --> 00:28:28.740
needs to look like.

00:28:28.740 --> 00:28:31.620
But yesterday we had some
really interesting discussions,

00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:32.790
very important discussions.

00:28:32.790 --> 00:28:36.960
We saw the final version
of the quality strategy.

00:28:36.960 --> 00:28:39.000
It needs now to go to
ministerial approval,

00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:41.203
but it's a huge amount of work,

00:28:41.203 --> 00:28:45.450
a very complicated topic
discussing the three major elements

00:28:45.450 --> 00:28:48.810
of safety, effectiveness,
in other words, outcomes

00:28:48.810 --> 00:28:50.460
and patient experience.

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:51.780
I think it's a really good document.

00:28:51.780 --> 00:28:53.850
It's got a brilliant
executive summary at the front

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:56.580
that really sets the tone.

00:28:56.580 --> 00:28:59.550
We saw the first modern service framework.

00:28:59.550 --> 00:29:04.550
These are under generation in
multiple clinical pathways.

00:29:04.590 --> 00:29:08.010
The one we saw was the sepsis
modern service framework

00:29:08.010 --> 00:29:10.650
and we thought it was
terrific, really high quality

00:29:10.650 --> 00:29:14.223
piece of work full of
ambition, full of innovation.

00:29:15.210 --> 00:29:17.880
We had an interesting discussion
about risk around quality,

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:21.570
and I think what I would say
is that we're in a better place

00:29:21.570 --> 00:29:24.600
because quality now is the
central organising principle

00:29:24.600 --> 00:29:28.920
of the NHS and this
quality strategy I think

00:29:28.920 --> 00:29:33.840
makes us all safer and added to which

00:29:33.840 --> 00:29:36.150
there's greater clarity of accountability

00:29:36.150 --> 00:29:39.420
for safety and quality
through the organisation.

00:29:39.420 --> 00:29:42.150
Last thing I'll mention, we
had an interesting discussion

00:29:42.150 --> 00:29:45.360
about neighbourhoods,
neighbourhood health centres,

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:46.770
and how they're going to be regulated.

00:29:46.770 --> 00:29:49.620
We didn't resolve it because
it's a complicated topic,

00:29:49.620 --> 00:29:52.593
but it's one that we'll
undoubtedly return to.

00:29:53.910 --> 00:29:58.260
- Okay, great. Thank you. Any
comments, questions on that?

00:29:58.260 --> 00:29:59.400
Okay, good.

00:29:59.400 --> 00:30:03.690
So hopefully it gives a sense
of the comprehensiveness

00:30:03.690 --> 00:30:05.640
really of the work that's happening

00:30:05.640 --> 00:30:07.980
and some of the depth of conversations

00:30:07.980 --> 00:30:11.520
and key part that we're doing
here overall is to ensure

00:30:11.520 --> 00:30:15.393
that all of that sits together
into a coherent whole.

00:30:16.530 --> 00:30:18.242
Jim, I'll hand over to you.

00:30:18.242 --> 00:30:20.490
- Thanks, Penny, so there's
a lot to cover that today.

00:30:20.490 --> 00:30:22.050
It feels like there's
an awful lot happening

00:30:22.050 --> 00:30:23.850
since we last met.

00:30:23.850 --> 00:30:27.630
So I'll try and do this quickly
and obviously colleagues

00:30:27.630 --> 00:30:29.340
just shout if there's any questions.

00:30:29.340 --> 00:30:30.900
First of all, on industrial action,

00:30:30.900 --> 00:30:32.160
I can feel the anxiety in the room

00:30:32.160 --> 00:30:34.050
about what I'm going to say
about industrial action.

00:30:34.050 --> 00:30:36.120
But it's not the first
time we've been sat here

00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:40.380
with a disappointment of having
to look into future strikes.

00:30:40.380 --> 00:30:42.933
I'd just say, Glen, I'm
sure I'll pick this up.

00:30:44.490 --> 00:30:48.600
Fantastic amount of effort and
work has gone in both sides

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:50.460
to try and reach agreement.

00:30:50.460 --> 00:30:53.280
And we felt very, very, very
close that we had a deal

00:30:53.280 --> 00:30:55.080
that could work for all parties.

00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:57.240
Incredibly disappointing
that it fell to bits

00:30:57.240 --> 00:31:00.270
at the last minute. I thought
it's actually not true.

00:31:00.270 --> 00:31:03.480
I think that our position changed

00:31:03.480 --> 00:31:06.082
during the negotiation,
but we are where we are.

00:31:06.082 --> 00:31:08.490
So we're going to have to
get ourselves organised

00:31:08.490 --> 00:31:12.180
about just navigating what's
probably a long period now

00:31:12.180 --> 00:31:15.960
of dispute and the NHS has
managed it well in the last year.

00:31:15.960 --> 00:31:18.030
We'll have to settle ourselves
into a bit of a rhythm

00:31:18.030 --> 00:31:21.780
where we're managing this
business as usual as best we can.

00:31:21.780 --> 00:31:24.450
As I say, Glen might
want to chip in later on.

00:31:24.450 --> 00:31:26.280
We are also at that horrible time of year

00:31:26.280 --> 00:31:28.380
where you're trying to finish a year well

00:31:28.380 --> 00:31:30.600
and start and set up next year

00:31:30.600 --> 00:31:32.670
and also increasingly set up future years

00:31:32.670 --> 00:31:35.370
as part of our medium
term planning processes

00:31:35.370 --> 00:31:38.070
has already been mentioned
a couple of times.

00:31:38.070 --> 00:31:40.620
So we're still in fighting distance

00:31:40.620 --> 00:31:42.180
on some of the key metrics.

00:31:42.180 --> 00:31:44.850
We've just had last
week's elective numbers.

00:31:44.850 --> 00:31:47.793
I can't say them because I'll
get into a lot of trouble.

00:31:48.690 --> 00:31:51.600
But we are inching awards,
getting where we're needed to be,

00:31:51.600 --> 00:31:52.890
or at least very close,

00:31:52.890 --> 00:31:55.650
and also on some of the other key metrics.

00:31:55.650 --> 00:31:58.830
I think to be at this position
at this time of the year,

00:31:58.830 --> 00:32:00.323
given everything that's been done,

00:32:00.323 --> 00:32:02.340
I think it's absolutely
incredible for the, you know,

00:32:02.340 --> 00:32:05.250
what the NHS has achieved
and colleagues here

00:32:05.250 --> 00:32:08.703
have helped lead through the year as well.

00:32:10.020 --> 00:32:14.490
Also we've all seen and been
involved with the horrors

00:32:14.490 --> 00:32:17.160
of the meningitis issue last week,

00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:20.070
and terrible tragedy for
those that lost their lives

00:32:20.070 --> 00:32:22.890
and the shock for the families involved

00:32:22.890 --> 00:32:24.804
and also the staff dealing with that.

00:32:24.804 --> 00:32:26.878
I would just say as well, a
lot of complexity in all that,

00:32:26.878 --> 00:32:29.940
the service really
responded very, very well

00:32:29.940 --> 00:32:32.670
in a very complex set of circumstances.

00:32:32.670 --> 00:32:36.480
In a fragile health system that
has its challenges as well,

00:32:36.480 --> 00:32:40.230
but, you know, thanks to Sarah
Jane, EPRR colleagues here

00:32:40.230 --> 00:32:42.570
but also out there in the
NHS and UKHSA as well,

00:32:42.570 --> 00:32:44.730
managing a very, very difficult situation.

00:32:44.730 --> 00:32:48.750
Hopefully we're coming out
of the other end of that.

00:32:49.650 --> 00:32:51.690
On planning, Elizabeth mentioned this,

00:32:51.690 --> 00:32:56.690
we are now close to having a
path that can work next year.

00:32:57.300 --> 00:32:58.650
We've got a bit of work to do

00:32:58.650 --> 00:33:01.140
on those that have stubborn problems,

00:33:01.140 --> 00:33:02.831
it's had a bit of coverage this week.

00:33:02.831 --> 00:33:04.770
We'll talk about improvement later on,

00:33:04.770 --> 00:33:07.620
and we've got a session on
Monday now with chief execs

00:33:07.620 --> 00:33:11.580
of NPIP organisations where
we can have a discussion

00:33:11.580 --> 00:33:13.620
about how we transition
into this new approach

00:33:13.620 --> 00:33:17.640
that was announced by secretary
of state yesterday as well.

00:33:17.640 --> 00:33:20.430
And in all of this, we
just had a good discussion

00:33:20.430 --> 00:33:23.190
before the meeting about the
tension between delivering

00:33:23.190 --> 00:33:26.940
day to day, grinding through
results as best we can,

00:33:26.940 --> 00:33:29.760
but making the big leaps,
the big steps that we need

00:33:29.760 --> 00:33:33.030
to make going through this next year

00:33:33.030 --> 00:33:35.790
and accelerating our rate of progress.

00:33:35.790 --> 00:33:38.030
So I think boards, you know,
spend a bit of time on this

00:33:38.030 --> 00:33:40.140
in the next few weeks to just think about

00:33:40.140 --> 00:33:44.040
where we can best help bring that to life

00:33:44.040 --> 00:33:45.540
for colleagues out there.

00:33:45.540 --> 00:33:47.160
And then also just to
quickly rattle through,

00:33:47.160 --> 00:33:49.530
as I said earlier on
as Andrew's mentioned,

00:33:49.530 --> 00:33:51.900
the delivery on the money this year

00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:54.430
being within fighting
distance on the key metrics

00:33:55.950 --> 00:33:58.950
given where we are now, getting
through industrial action

00:33:58.950 --> 00:34:02.220
through the year, managing
winter, the 10 year plan,

00:34:02.220 --> 00:34:04.200
and then very importantly,

00:34:04.200 --> 00:34:08.070
British social attitude
survey results this week.

00:34:08.070 --> 00:34:11.370
Not perfect, still a lot
to do, still things in that

00:34:11.370 --> 00:34:14.010
we would be unhappy about,
but a big improvement

00:34:14.010 --> 00:34:18.540
after two really dreadful
years of decline.

00:34:18.540 --> 00:34:20.400
And then just very finally,

00:34:20.400 --> 00:34:22.230
again, as already being mentioned,

00:34:22.230 --> 00:34:25.500
we've got a lot of
colleagues leaving here,

00:34:25.500 --> 00:34:28.140
ICBs are that the part of the service.

00:34:28.140 --> 00:34:29.250
Some have already left.

00:34:29.250 --> 00:34:30.957
I've said goodbye to colleagues
I've known and worked with

00:34:30.957 --> 00:34:33.150
for 30 odd years.

00:34:33.150 --> 00:34:34.920
I met a colleague yesterday here

00:34:34.920 --> 00:34:38.580
who really wanted to
end a long career well,

00:34:38.580 --> 00:34:43.580
feeling good about their
work, the organisation,

00:34:43.860 --> 00:34:44.867
their contribution to the NHS,

00:34:44.867 --> 00:34:46.440
and we should really acknowledge

00:34:46.440 --> 00:34:49.860
this is a huge change, and
there's a lot of individuals,

00:34:49.860 --> 00:34:52.410
a lot of people involved, and
we can't lose sight of that

00:34:52.410 --> 00:34:56.640
through a pretty difficult process.

00:34:56.640 --> 00:34:58.110
So I would like to thank them all

00:34:58.110 --> 00:34:59.280
for what they've done.

00:34:59.280 --> 00:35:01.320
Their patience through
a pretty messy year,

00:35:01.320 --> 00:35:04.770
horrible process of
trying to reach agreement

00:35:04.770 --> 00:35:08.870
for voluntary redundancy, but
hopefully everyone can move on

00:35:08.870 --> 00:35:10.590
to this next stage.

00:35:10.590 --> 00:35:13.980
So big thanks from me, I'm
sure others in the board.

00:35:13.980 --> 00:35:16.770
And then we've got a bit of
a change going on in execs

00:35:16.770 --> 00:35:19.260
obviously as well, as we shared last week

00:35:19.260 --> 00:35:20.460
with colleagues coming and going.

00:35:20.460 --> 00:35:23.340
And again, just thank David
for everything he's done

00:35:23.340 --> 00:35:27.420
when we took them hostage last
year to make a contribution.

00:35:27.420 --> 00:35:30.180
We've got another part of the
hostage release scheme going

00:35:30.180 --> 00:35:32.700
on over the next few months
as colleagues go back.

00:35:32.700 --> 00:35:33.533
None of this all joined.

00:35:33.533 --> 00:35:35.910
So good few jobs we'll be
advertising the next few weeks

00:35:35.910 --> 00:35:39.360
to demonstrate this is more
sustainable making permanent

00:35:39.360 --> 00:35:42.420
recruitment choices
about the future as well.

00:35:42.420 --> 00:35:46.200
So that'll power up in
this next couple of weeks.

00:35:46.200 --> 00:35:49.293
So it's been a really, really busy year,

00:35:50.550 --> 00:35:53.370
really, really incredibly
busy last few weeks.

00:35:53.370 --> 00:35:56.760
What's the thing with
this that makes it good

00:35:56.760 --> 00:35:59.340
but also really painful
as you're never done.

00:35:59.340 --> 00:36:01.680
Yeah, there's never an easy point ahead,

00:36:01.680 --> 00:36:06.060
but I think we should absolutely
acknowledge, recognise,

00:36:06.060 --> 00:36:09.810
and congratulate what the
NHS has managed to deliver

00:36:09.810 --> 00:36:11.613
in a pretty difficult year.

00:36:12.450 --> 00:36:15.462
- Okay, Glen, did you want to come back

00:36:15.462 --> 00:36:16.295
in on the strikes?

00:36:16.295 --> 00:36:21.000
- Yeah, if I may, I mean, just
to echo Jim's comments there,

00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:23.763
incredibly disappointed that
we've reached this point.

00:36:24.690 --> 00:36:28.650
We put forward a, what I
feel is a really good offer

00:36:28.650 --> 00:36:33.240
to resident doctors huge amount
of work from Meghana and I

00:36:33.240 --> 00:36:36.420
and other colleagues in
the negotiating team,

00:36:36.420 --> 00:36:38.550
and actually from the
negotiating team of the BMA.

00:36:38.550 --> 00:36:42.240
So we pulled together a
really good joint proposal

00:36:42.240 --> 00:36:47.240
that covered jobs, pay,
wider contract reforms,

00:36:47.430 --> 00:36:49.950
and every single issue that was put to us,

00:36:49.950 --> 00:36:52.650
we addressed through a
rather large document

00:36:52.650 --> 00:36:54.000
that came together for that.

00:36:54.000 --> 00:36:59.000
So when the BMA negotiating
team put it to their committee,

00:36:59.250 --> 00:37:01.110
I was really shocked that the committee

00:37:01.110 --> 00:37:04.323
decided not to even put
it to their members.

00:37:05.820 --> 00:37:10.820
So as a result of that we will
seek to put the details of it

00:37:11.910 --> 00:37:13.200
into the public domain

00:37:13.200 --> 00:37:15.180
because I think it's
important the resident doctors

00:37:15.180 --> 00:37:19.590
see the lengths that we have
gone to address their concerns

00:37:19.590 --> 00:37:22.620
and for the wider NHS
actually to see that,

00:37:22.620 --> 00:37:25.563
but also to see what we're
going to lose as a result.

00:37:26.580 --> 00:37:27.780
And one of the immediate things

00:37:27.780 --> 00:37:32.610
is we were about to recruit to
a thousand new trading posts

00:37:32.610 --> 00:37:34.320
and to see that not moving forward

00:37:34.320 --> 00:37:35.920
is really, really disappointing.

00:37:37.170 --> 00:37:42.170
- Yeah, so there's a loss to
the resident doctor community,

00:37:42.540 --> 00:37:45.333
as you say, and you're
going to publish that then?

00:37:46.339 --> 00:37:48.840
- So we will share the wider offer

00:37:48.840 --> 00:37:50.100
including some of the contractual

00:37:50.100 --> 00:37:51.630
improvements that we are suggesting.

00:37:51.630 --> 00:37:55.890
- Okay, but there's also
obviously a loss to our patients,

00:37:55.890 --> 00:38:00.210
our users and their families,
and indeed to the other staff,

00:38:00.210 --> 00:38:01.860
because I'm noting that these strikes

00:38:01.860 --> 00:38:04.923
will be over the holiday
period again, is that right?

00:38:04.923 --> 00:38:06.673
- [Jim] Yeah.
- Okay.

00:38:07.830 --> 00:38:10.980
Yeah, it's really
disappointing for patients.

00:38:10.980 --> 00:38:14.130
I mean, this is at a point where we know

00:38:14.130 --> 00:38:16.110
we'll be at a busy stage again,

00:38:16.110 --> 00:38:21.090
so it feels like it's kind of
trying to push maximum harm

00:38:21.090 --> 00:38:23.100
onto patients, but we will
make sure that doesn't happen

00:38:23.100 --> 00:38:24.123
an NHS.

00:38:25.110 --> 00:38:27.120
But also all the other
colleagues in the NHS

00:38:27.120 --> 00:38:29.730
who will step in and cover
for resident doctors,

00:38:29.730 --> 00:38:31.830
it's hard for them also.

00:38:31.830 --> 00:38:33.270
- Yeah, okay.

00:38:33.270 --> 00:38:34.380
- And not just on that page,

00:38:34.380 --> 00:38:36.870
we'll stand up the usual
arrangements next week

00:38:36.870 --> 00:38:39.003
to prepare as we usually do.

00:38:39.990 --> 00:38:44.010
We've treated every strike
so far as the last one

00:38:44.010 --> 00:38:46.264
and really, I think, you know,
managed to really mitigate

00:38:46.264 --> 00:38:49.080
the impact on patients
to a significant degree.

00:38:49.080 --> 00:38:51.330
We want to keep that going as best we can,

00:38:51.330 --> 00:38:53.400
but we have to acknowledge
that we're going to have people

00:38:53.400 --> 00:38:55.380
just disrupting their
leave every few weeks

00:38:55.380 --> 00:38:57.930
and being able to get on with their lives.

00:38:57.930 --> 00:38:59.730
So there's a balance
we've now got to strike

00:38:59.730 --> 00:39:04.730
if we assume we're now in at
least a medium mid-distance run

00:39:04.890 --> 00:39:06.540
rather than a sprint,

00:39:06.540 --> 00:39:09.090
it's probably long
distance if we're honest

00:39:09.090 --> 00:39:10.710
so we've got to get ourselves organised

00:39:10.710 --> 00:39:13.050
to be able to sustain that
over a long period of time.

00:39:13.050 --> 00:39:17.790
- Yeah. So I think we will
need to see what that means.

00:39:17.790 --> 00:39:21.570
How do we move into a
different way of working

00:39:21.570 --> 00:39:23.120
that sustains this, as you say?

00:39:24.240 --> 00:39:27.150
Okay. Well, very, very
disappointing. Thank you.

00:39:27.150 --> 00:39:29.400
I know many people around this table

00:39:29.400 --> 00:39:34.380
put in a lot of time to it
and we'll see where we get to.

00:39:34.380 --> 00:39:37.500
And also to echo Jim, the
thanks to the many colleagues

00:39:37.500 --> 00:39:38.910
who will be leaving us

00:39:38.910 --> 00:39:40.860
through the voluntary redundancy programme

00:39:40.860 --> 00:39:45.183
for their work over the, in
some cases over many years.

00:39:46.200 --> 00:39:51.150
Okay, let's go on to the wider
performance report, David?

00:39:51.150 --> 00:39:52.440
- Thank you, Chair.

00:39:52.440 --> 00:39:54.513
And really just to begin in the way

00:39:54.513 --> 00:39:56.670
that Jim just ended there
by thanking colleagues

00:39:56.670 --> 00:39:59.280
across the NHS for all of our hard work

00:39:59.280 --> 00:40:00.507
as we approach the end of this year

00:40:00.507 --> 00:40:02.040
and I give a performance report,

00:40:02.040 --> 00:40:05.100
which looks really at the month
of February predominantly,

00:40:05.100 --> 00:40:08.130
but I'll try and touch on
just a couple of other points

00:40:08.130 --> 00:40:10.260
that we know of towards
the end of this year.

00:40:10.260 --> 00:40:12.630
So you'll see in this report,
we've done a couple of things.

00:40:12.630 --> 00:40:15.470
Firstly, we've included
a summary of performance.

00:40:15.470 --> 00:40:18.000
So I won't go into things in great detail,

00:40:18.000 --> 00:40:20.490
but we've also added six more areas

00:40:20.490 --> 00:40:21.870
that the board had talked to us about

00:40:21.870 --> 00:40:23.010
over previous meetings.

00:40:23.010 --> 00:40:24.750
So community waits is a proportion

00:40:24.750 --> 00:40:26.643
of 52 week waits by region.

00:40:28.170 --> 00:40:31.860
Child and young adult community
waits split by specialty,

00:40:31.860 --> 00:40:34.740
healthy life expectancy
both by male and female,

00:40:34.740 --> 00:40:37.950
emergency admissions for over 65s,

00:40:37.950 --> 00:40:39.360
and then the estimates

00:40:39.360 --> 00:40:42.540
of potentially avoidable
emergency admissions.

00:40:42.540 --> 00:40:44.250
So I'll just summarise in brief form

00:40:44.250 --> 00:40:45.450
maybe in three categories,

00:40:45.450 --> 00:40:47.610
those areas where we've
seen improved performance

00:40:47.610 --> 00:40:49.590
in particularly over the
course of this report,

00:40:49.590 --> 00:40:51.300
so looking back on February,

00:40:51.300 --> 00:40:52.890
areas where we are perhaps faced

00:40:52.890 --> 00:40:55.290
a little bit more
challenge, and then finally,

00:40:55.290 --> 00:40:56.940
just where we are in the month of February

00:40:56.940 --> 00:40:59.430
on some of those key access targets

00:40:59.430 --> 00:41:03.300
that we know and talk about
an awful lot across the NHS.

00:41:03.300 --> 00:41:06.630
So firstly on improved
performance you will see that

00:41:06.630 --> 00:41:10.200
we continue to see improving
performance, small numbers,

00:41:10.200 --> 00:41:12.630
but improving performance
in a big area of focus,

00:41:12.630 --> 00:41:15.030
which is the adult inpatients with autism

00:41:15.030 --> 00:41:17.340
or learning disability reducing.

00:41:17.340 --> 00:41:19.890
And that's a very positive
reflection of the good work

00:41:19.890 --> 00:41:22.500
that's taking place across the NHS.

00:41:22.500 --> 00:41:25.410
You'll see a number of
improvements in areas of screening,

00:41:25.410 --> 00:41:29.340
cervical, breast, bowel, where
we are seeing small upticks

00:41:29.340 --> 00:41:31.410
of improvement against, again,

00:41:31.410 --> 00:41:33.150
metrics which are further away,

00:41:33.150 --> 00:41:35.400
and that's the way, of course,
it works with screening,

00:41:35.400 --> 00:41:38.190
but it's positive to
see those improvements.

00:41:38.190 --> 00:41:41.610
Continued improvements in
mental health beds out of area,

00:41:41.610 --> 00:41:44.640
which we know from a patient
and family experience

00:41:44.640 --> 00:41:46.680
is particularly important.

00:41:46.680 --> 00:41:50.730
As is crisis care, face-to-face
contact within 24 hours

00:41:50.730 --> 00:41:53.220
where we're seeing improvement
in the amount of people

00:41:53.220 --> 00:41:56.850
that are able to access
that vitally important care.

00:41:56.850 --> 00:41:59.610
We talked a couple of meetings
ago around the importance

00:41:59.610 --> 00:42:04.230
of prevention being highlighted,
so it's positive to see

00:42:04.230 --> 00:42:06.300
some of the good work
and improvement in areas

00:42:06.300 --> 00:42:09.450
of hypertension and
cholesterol management,

00:42:09.450 --> 00:42:12.390
which has continued to see
a year-on-year improvement

00:42:12.390 --> 00:42:16.170
and the statistics are there
and obviously predominantly

00:42:16.170 --> 00:42:18.630
provided by our colleagues
in primary care.

00:42:18.630 --> 00:42:20.460
So important to reflect the good work

00:42:20.460 --> 00:42:22.140
that we're seeing there.

00:42:22.140 --> 00:42:25.110
And then finally, continuing
healthcare, 28-day completion,

00:42:25.110 --> 00:42:28.680
we improved again an upwards of 1.3%,

00:42:28.680 --> 00:42:32.160
which again shows the sort of broader look

00:42:32.160 --> 00:42:34.770
at the way we're managing
healthcare pathways.

00:42:34.770 --> 00:42:37.260
So a number of key areas where
we're seeing improvement.

00:42:37.260 --> 00:42:39.210
As I said, I come on at
the end just to talk about

00:42:39.210 --> 00:42:41.250
some of the key access targets.

00:42:41.250 --> 00:42:44.190
There are a number of areas
Andrew's already touched on them

00:42:44.190 --> 00:42:48.660
and Jim a little that we are
still reporting as challenging.

00:42:48.660 --> 00:42:50.970
Healthy life expectancy already discussed

00:42:50.970 --> 00:42:53.400
is probably the most significant of those.

00:42:53.400 --> 00:42:56.460
As you can see, that continues to decline,

00:42:56.460 --> 00:42:59.010
and is an area that
outside of this meeting,

00:42:59.010 --> 00:43:02.940
we've discussed looking
at a deeper dive into

00:43:02.940 --> 00:43:05.430
and perhaps bringing back to
board in a lot more detail.

00:43:05.430 --> 00:43:07.530
And I think we're looking
at commissioning colleagues

00:43:07.530 --> 00:43:10.440
externally to help us with
some expertise and advice

00:43:10.440 --> 00:43:12.360
in understanding this.

00:43:12.360 --> 00:43:16.590
MMR vaccination rates, we
reported at the last board

00:43:16.590 --> 00:43:18.030
that our uptake remains below

00:43:18.030 --> 00:43:21.450
the World Health Organisation thresholds

00:43:21.450 --> 00:43:24.870
and therefore posing a
continued outbreak risk.

00:43:24.870 --> 00:43:27.300
Given what we've seen a very
different issue, of course,

00:43:27.300 --> 00:43:31.530
but with meningitis, one
hopes that public awareness

00:43:31.530 --> 00:43:34.050
is heightened again, and we
need to use that opportunity

00:43:34.050 --> 00:43:37.050
to talk about the absolute
value of vaccination,

00:43:37.050 --> 00:43:39.570
but at the moment, those
rates are still standing

00:43:39.570 --> 00:43:41.550
at about 83 and a half percent,

00:43:41.550 --> 00:43:44.190
so below where we need them to be.

00:43:44.190 --> 00:43:46.470
Community mental health satisfaction rates

00:43:46.470 --> 00:43:50.310
has seen a very slight decline
over the period of this time,

00:43:50.310 --> 00:43:54.030
and you can see across two of
our main core cancer targets,

00:43:54.030 --> 00:43:55.590
the faster diagnostic standard,

00:43:55.590 --> 00:43:57.600
and also the 62 day target,

00:43:57.600 --> 00:44:01.020
small declines over that Christmas period.

00:44:01.020 --> 00:44:04.170
But what I would say having
managed cancer performance

00:44:04.170 --> 00:44:06.870
for many, many years,
it is always challenging

00:44:06.870 --> 00:44:09.420
when you pick up after
the Christmas period,

00:44:09.420 --> 00:44:12.270
because just by the
very nature of activity

00:44:12.270 --> 00:44:14.820
over that period, there
tends to be a bit of a dip.

00:44:16.290 --> 00:44:18.030
There are a number of
other targets highlighted,

00:44:18.030 --> 00:44:21.030
and just to a point that I
think Andrew was raising early

00:44:21.030 --> 00:44:22.980
around staff engagement and experience,

00:44:22.980 --> 00:44:26.760
we've tried to include a number of areas

00:44:26.760 --> 00:44:29.730
which picked out of the
staff survey most recently.

00:44:29.730 --> 00:44:31.680
And disappointingly, a couple of those,

00:44:31.680 --> 00:44:35.880
I've seen a small decline,
raising concerns being one

00:44:35.880 --> 00:44:37.680
always being a learning organisation.

00:44:37.680 --> 00:44:39.990
And then finally,
probably most significant

00:44:39.990 --> 00:44:41.430
for NHS providers like mine,

00:44:41.430 --> 00:44:43.350
we look at the engagement theme score,

00:44:43.350 --> 00:44:47.160
which has seen a small dip
in the reported details

00:44:47.160 --> 00:44:48.510
from last time.

00:44:48.510 --> 00:44:51.840
So just finally on the key
performance indicators,

00:44:51.840 --> 00:44:54.450
again we're really looking at February

00:44:54.450 --> 00:44:56.760
and in some instances
for elective and cancer

00:44:56.760 --> 00:44:59.490
at January here, so a
little bit of a look back

00:44:59.490 --> 00:45:02.670
but you can see that UEC
attendances over that month

00:45:02.670 --> 00:45:04.680
continue to rise.

00:45:04.680 --> 00:45:09.000
You can see from the data
that there is improvement

00:45:09.000 --> 00:45:13.290
in our four hour target
and as Jim mentioned

00:45:13.290 --> 00:45:15.810
the most recent data would
suggest good improvement

00:45:15.810 --> 00:45:18.450
in that area, but of course
we still have some way to go

00:45:18.450 --> 00:45:21.300
before the end of the financial year.

00:45:21.300 --> 00:45:24.810
And the percentage of patients
spending over 12 hours

00:45:24.810 --> 00:45:29.580
in ED from arrival, again,
we're starting to improve

00:45:29.580 --> 00:45:31.860
only by a few percent,
but that's all important

00:45:31.860 --> 00:45:34.350
when we think about the
numbers that equates to

00:45:34.350 --> 00:45:36.840
in terms of patient experience.

00:45:36.840 --> 00:45:39.840
On elective, as Jim has mentioned

00:45:39.840 --> 00:45:43.020
all colleagues across the NHS
are working incredibly hard

00:45:43.020 --> 00:45:44.910
to make sure our patients
can be looked after

00:45:44.910 --> 00:45:47.220
and we can achieve the target that's set.

00:45:47.220 --> 00:45:50.500
The January figures show a
decreasing waiting list position

00:45:51.600 --> 00:45:55.810
against the month previous
and the month last year

00:45:56.670 --> 00:46:00.420
and we are continuing to make
good progress on long waiters

00:46:00.420 --> 00:46:04.037
over 52 weeks certainly
against the previous months

00:46:04.037 --> 00:46:05.430
and the previous year.

00:46:05.430 --> 00:46:07.440
You'll see some improvements in cancer

00:46:07.440 --> 00:46:09.510
when we look back on a year's progress,

00:46:09.510 --> 00:46:11.760
which again is important
when you're looking at

00:46:11.760 --> 00:46:16.230
large scale change and then finally

00:46:16.230 --> 00:46:18.660
minimal change in areas like diagnostics,

00:46:18.660 --> 00:46:20.370
but that's probably reflected better

00:46:20.370 --> 00:46:23.100
in the overall cancer targets.

00:46:23.100 --> 00:46:25.980
So we await the end of this year,

00:46:25.980 --> 00:46:27.540
the good work that people are reporting

00:46:27.540 --> 00:46:29.220
and that will be reported I think probably

00:46:29.220 --> 00:46:31.320
Penny in the next board meeting.

00:46:31.320 --> 00:46:33.120
But very happy to take questions

00:46:33.120 --> 00:46:34.620
and as always conscious,

00:46:34.620 --> 00:46:37.520
my expert colleagues have sat
round the table to redirect.

00:46:38.880 --> 00:46:39.750
- Thank you, David.

00:46:39.750 --> 00:46:43.800
So I think just to take
us back to the point,

00:46:43.800 --> 00:46:46.233
Andrew you started off
on, life expectancy,

00:46:47.490 --> 00:46:49.440
you mentioned commissioning
a piece of work.

00:46:49.440 --> 00:46:52.410
I think given there's
quite a big focus of this

00:46:52.410 --> 00:46:55.320
in the quality strategy, it's important

00:46:55.320 --> 00:46:57.903
that it does link to
that quality strategy.

00:46:59.580 --> 00:47:02.910
I was also kind of raised though that it,

00:47:02.910 --> 00:47:06.030
yes, primary prevention,
wide determinants of health

00:47:06.030 --> 00:47:09.930
will be critical around
improving life expectancy,

00:47:09.930 --> 00:47:13.380
but how we manage people with illness,

00:47:13.380 --> 00:47:15.360
so particularly with diabetes,

00:47:15.360 --> 00:47:18.990
with cardiovascular disease
is absolutely crucial,

00:47:18.990 --> 00:47:23.990
and our metrics on those are
pretty poor, to be blunt.

00:47:24.480 --> 00:47:28.290
I mean, they're quite
consistently below 50%.

00:47:28.290 --> 00:47:30.540
So that feels like that should really be

00:47:30.540 --> 00:47:31.680
a big area of focus.

00:47:31.680 --> 00:47:34.260
So I'm not necessarily
saying to respond to it now,

00:47:34.260 --> 00:47:37.080
but, you know, that's the
bit that jumps off the page,

00:47:37.080 --> 00:47:39.540
and hopefully the modern
service frameworks

00:47:39.540 --> 00:47:41.640
will help to address some of that,

00:47:41.640 --> 00:47:43.500
but I'm not quite sure all.

00:47:43.500 --> 00:47:47.070
So just wonder whether we
could keep quite relentlessly

00:47:47.070 --> 00:47:50.703
looking at that and maybe
even adding a bit more.

00:47:51.720 --> 00:47:54.000
I had a question about the 11 trusts

00:47:54.000 --> 00:47:57.060
with relatively high
standardised mortality rates.

00:47:57.060 --> 00:48:00.780
What do we then do when we see that?

00:48:00.780 --> 00:48:03.930
And then my final one
was given all of this,

00:48:03.930 --> 00:48:06.270
should we start to measure how we...

00:48:06.270 --> 00:48:09.270
Which would essentially bring
together everything across

00:48:09.270 --> 00:48:13.323
performance what level of
healthcare value we are achieving.

00:48:14.160 --> 00:48:16.050
So are we spending our money

00:48:16.050 --> 00:48:18.870
on the most impactful interventions

00:48:18.870 --> 00:48:21.420
to deliver improvements in life expectancy

00:48:21.420 --> 00:48:22.470
and quality of life?

00:48:22.470 --> 00:48:24.253
And maybe that comes
out to the piece of work

00:48:24.253 --> 00:48:26.550
and out of the quality strategy,

00:48:26.550 --> 00:48:28.830
but that might be something
that we could have a go

00:48:28.830 --> 00:48:32.580
at having an overarching
almost score ourselves

00:48:32.580 --> 00:48:35.400
on how well we're doing on
that, just as a thought.

00:48:35.400 --> 00:48:36.240
So those are a few from me,

00:48:36.240 --> 00:48:38.610
but let's take a few others to go around.

00:48:38.610 --> 00:48:41.070
So I've got Ravi and then Sam

00:48:41.070 --> 00:48:43.023
and then Robert and then Louise.

00:48:43.980 --> 00:48:46.290
- Thank you, there's a couple
of slides on the variation

00:48:46.290 --> 00:48:49.410
in performance so the 12 hour performance

00:48:49.410 --> 00:48:52.203
and ambulance wait times.

00:48:53.160 --> 00:48:56.043
I just wondered how stable
are the bottom 10 providers?

00:48:57.535 --> 00:48:59.100
Are they there for a long period of time?

00:48:59.100 --> 00:49:01.770
Second, do we have enough
granular data to understand

00:49:01.770 --> 00:49:03.480
what is driving that variation such

00:49:03.480 --> 00:49:06.570
that we can take some
actionable steps with them

00:49:06.570 --> 00:49:08.340
and then thirdly, you
know, are we providing that

00:49:08.340 --> 00:49:11.010
intensive accountability and support

00:49:11.010 --> 00:49:12.780
to try and address them?

00:49:13.950 --> 00:49:15.063
- Okay, great. Sam?

00:49:16.560 --> 00:49:18.630
- So it's on your theme Penny,

00:49:18.630 --> 00:49:21.090
of healthy life Expectancy.

00:49:21.090 --> 00:49:24.900
Figures I've seen by 2040 is
the healthy life expectancy

00:49:24.900 --> 00:49:29.280
between rich and poor is going
to be 20 years different.

00:49:29.280 --> 00:49:32.790
So in deprived areas like
some of the areas I work,

00:49:32.790 --> 00:49:36.060
you can expect to hit
chronic disease at 50.

00:49:36.060 --> 00:49:39.330
If you're in a wealthier area,
it will be the age of 70.

00:49:39.330 --> 00:49:41.730
It would be really nice to have that

00:49:41.730 --> 00:49:45.540
within some of the data,
not just to highlight it,

00:49:45.540 --> 00:49:48.480
but also actually that
will help us tease down,

00:49:48.480 --> 00:49:53.070
as Penny rightly says, what's
within our gift to change

00:49:53.070 --> 00:49:55.590
and what isn't and for those things

00:49:55.590 --> 00:49:56.970
that are not within our gift actually,

00:49:56.970 --> 00:50:01.420
how do we land them in
other parts of the system

00:50:03.240 --> 00:50:04.590
in order to get some way forward.

00:50:04.590 --> 00:50:07.440
And then on the MMR thing,

00:50:07.440 --> 00:50:09.300
which has been a struggle over many years,

00:50:09.300 --> 00:50:12.150
there has been times where
we've been much more successful.

00:50:12.150 --> 00:50:13.590
What sort of learning are we doing

00:50:13.590 --> 00:50:16.620
from international comparators?

00:50:16.620 --> 00:50:19.710
In other words, what are
they doing in other systems

00:50:19.710 --> 00:50:21.453
that are getting the higher rates?

00:50:23.340 --> 00:50:24.930
- Robert?

00:50:24.930 --> 00:50:28.380
- Yeah, no, it's really,
really helpful report David,

00:50:28.380 --> 00:50:29.880
and some very positive things,

00:50:29.880 --> 00:50:32.790
but I wanted to zone in on hypertension.

00:50:32.790 --> 00:50:35.580
So, I mean, two of the biggest killers

00:50:35.580 --> 00:50:38.130
are cancer and cardiovascular.

00:50:38.130 --> 00:50:40.890
And one obvious preventive measure

00:50:40.890 --> 00:50:42.303
is blood pressure control.

00:50:43.138 --> 00:50:44.490
So there is a slight improvement.

00:50:44.490 --> 00:50:49.320
I accept that, but if you
accept that 33% of hypertension

00:50:49.320 --> 00:50:51.960
goes undiagnosed in the community,

00:50:51.960 --> 00:50:55.890
and if 50% of that is diagnosed
is inadequately managed,

00:50:55.890 --> 00:50:58.410
that means about 50% of
people with blood pressure

00:50:58.410 --> 00:51:00.180
are not being properly treated.

00:51:00.180 --> 00:51:02.490
So there's a really attractive target

00:51:02.490 --> 00:51:05.400
if we want to shift the
dial and it's something

00:51:05.400 --> 00:51:07.680
that I would hope
neighbourhood health centres

00:51:07.680 --> 00:51:09.000
will zone in on.

00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:12.240
So I just wanted to ensure
there was no complacency

00:51:12.240 --> 00:51:14.790
about blood pressure because
it's such a big problem

00:51:14.790 --> 00:51:16.140
and a soluble one, I think.

00:51:17.670 --> 00:51:20.550
- So Jim, you mentioned the
British Social Attitude Survey

00:51:20.550 --> 00:51:23.730
and the slight uptick
there in satisfaction,

00:51:23.730 --> 00:51:25.560
which is really good
news after so many years

00:51:25.560 --> 00:51:29.640
of it being so low and declining again.

00:51:29.640 --> 00:51:33.000
However, the actual satisfaction total

00:51:33.000 --> 00:51:34.590
is still very low, isn't it?

00:51:34.590 --> 00:51:39.590
And what we've got in terms
of the experience metric,

00:51:39.780 --> 00:51:42.307
there's only four metrics and experience,

00:51:42.307 --> 00:51:44.850
and we've discussed this
a few times, haven't we,

00:51:44.850 --> 00:51:47.940
is that when you ask people
what matters to them most about

00:51:47.940 --> 00:51:49.470
how satisfied they are with the NHS,

00:51:49.470 --> 00:51:54.470
they say access to services A&amp;E waits

00:51:54.510 --> 00:51:58.440
and how people are
communicated with by the NHS.

00:51:58.440 --> 00:52:01.170
And what we still don't
have here are any metrics

00:52:01.170 --> 00:52:04.110
around communication and how easy it is

00:52:04.110 --> 00:52:05.840
to kind of navigate the NHS.

00:52:05.840 --> 00:52:09.270
So I know Duncan and the new
director of patient experience

00:52:09.270 --> 00:52:13.200
will have plans around
that but I really feel like

00:52:13.200 --> 00:52:14.250
we should be speeding that up

00:52:14.250 --> 00:52:18.060
so we get a really clear
picture of experience.

00:52:18.060 --> 00:52:19.380
And equally on GP access,

00:52:19.380 --> 00:52:23.220
I've seen it has improved a
little bit, which is great,

00:52:23.220 --> 00:52:24.900
but again, people still feel

00:52:24.900 --> 00:52:28.110
that that is a significantly
difficult thing to do.

00:52:28.110 --> 00:52:32.020
So I know Amanda has been
doing a phenomenal job with GPs

00:52:33.000 --> 00:52:35.820
to improve that through the
contracts and other measures,

00:52:35.820 --> 00:52:38.453
but we need to keep up
the momentum on that

00:52:38.453 --> 00:52:41.520
and the focus on that in
order for that to continue

00:52:41.520 --> 00:52:43.020
to improve over the next year.

00:52:44.730 --> 00:52:46.110
- [Penny] Paul?

00:52:46.110 --> 00:52:47.760
- I just wanted to go back to
what you said earlier, Penny,

00:52:47.760 --> 00:52:50.483
about the relationship between
strategy and performance.

00:52:51.330 --> 00:52:53.670
All of this discussion around
healthy life Expectancy,

00:52:53.670 --> 00:52:55.410
reducing inequalities in
healthy life Expectancy,

00:52:55.410 --> 00:52:58.350
that is our core job
that is what our strategy

00:52:58.350 --> 00:53:00.540
should be aimed at and as we look through

00:53:00.540 --> 00:53:02.520
how we're doing from a
performance perspective,

00:53:02.520 --> 00:53:04.410
all of those things are inputs and outputs

00:53:04.410 --> 00:53:06.150
towards that goal.

00:53:06.150 --> 00:53:08.550
So I would just say, I think
it relates very, very strongly

00:53:08.550 --> 00:53:10.500
to the work we're doing
to try and get ourselves

00:53:10.500 --> 00:53:13.920
with a outcomes focused view
on ten year plan delivery.

00:53:13.920 --> 00:53:16.680
If we feel like we need to
go out for additional support

00:53:16.680 --> 00:53:18.810
on some technical analysis somewhere,

00:53:18.810 --> 00:53:20.070
then we should have that discussion,

00:53:20.070 --> 00:53:21.780
but actually this is our core job

00:53:21.780 --> 00:53:23.940
and I'm keen to make sure
that we're actually owning

00:53:23.940 --> 00:53:26.490
and doing that work properly internally.

00:53:26.490 --> 00:53:28.470
I think it links also
some discussions we've had

00:53:28.470 --> 00:53:30.960
with the chief medical officer
about how we understand

00:53:30.960 --> 00:53:32.790
how we are doing as a health
system at any one point,

00:53:32.790 --> 00:53:34.740
which I think comes back to
your point about value as well.

00:53:34.740 --> 00:53:38.370
So just a strong push
to hold us, ourselves,

00:53:38.370 --> 00:53:40.440
my directorate to account
for being able to,

00:53:40.440 --> 00:53:42.900
I think, lead that work and we can discuss

00:53:42.900 --> 00:53:44.740
how we join up best to do it.

00:53:44.740 --> 00:53:47.310
- Okay, so I've got Amanda and then Claire

00:53:47.310 --> 00:53:49.083
and then we'll head back to David.

00:53:49.960 --> 00:53:53.193
- So just on, I think Robert's
point about hypertension.

00:53:54.630 --> 00:53:57.270
One of the things we are
doing to try to increase

00:53:57.270 --> 00:53:59.910
our identification of
undiagnosed hypertension

00:53:59.910 --> 00:54:04.110
is giving open access to
pharmacies for testing

00:54:04.110 --> 00:54:07.800
and pharmacies this year have
significantly overachieved

00:54:07.800 --> 00:54:11.370
on the numbers we expected to
see, so that's quite positive.

00:54:11.370 --> 00:54:14.040
We obviously need to work at translating

00:54:14.040 --> 00:54:16.470
that into better managed hypertension.

00:54:16.470 --> 00:54:20.070
On Louisa's point, the health
insight survey that the ONS do

00:54:20.070 --> 00:54:23.400
for us every four weeks,
which isn't big enough

00:54:23.400 --> 00:54:26.823
to give us the degree of
detail at very local level,

00:54:27.840 --> 00:54:30.450
but gives us information very frequently,

00:54:30.450 --> 00:54:33.750
does ask a question about are
you on an NHS waiting list,

00:54:33.750 --> 00:54:37.050
how happy are you with the
communication you receive

00:54:37.050 --> 00:54:41.100
and does give us some insight
not particularly positive,

00:54:41.100 --> 00:54:42.630
but it does.

00:54:42.630 --> 00:54:43.980
- [Penny] Okay.

00:54:43.980 --> 00:54:46.180
- So we'll build on the importance

00:54:47.496 --> 00:54:49.770
of the management of long-term conditions.

00:54:49.770 --> 00:54:54.510
So the longer you can stop
people from collecting

00:54:54.510 --> 00:54:56.370
more long-term conditions and ending up

00:54:56.370 --> 00:54:58.500
with multiple long-term conditions,

00:54:58.500 --> 00:55:01.259
the more we will increase
the healthy life expectancy.

00:55:01.259 --> 00:55:02.490
Yeah, so the thing we
haven't talked about is,

00:55:02.490 --> 00:55:03.600
well, the neighbourhood health framework

00:55:03.600 --> 00:55:05.040
was published last week.

00:55:05.040 --> 00:55:07.740
And within that, we've actually,

00:55:07.740 --> 00:55:10.860
one of the main aims of that
is to improve the management

00:55:10.860 --> 00:55:13.170
of long-term conditions for people.

00:55:13.170 --> 00:55:16.260
So we've got the eight step care processes

00:55:16.260 --> 00:55:19.080
we're able to increase the
performance of that by 10%

00:55:19.080 --> 00:55:21.270
and we'll continue to
focus on hypertension

00:55:21.270 --> 00:55:23.790
and in particular case finding.

00:55:23.790 --> 00:55:28.080
So we've got, using our
usual assurance mechanisms

00:55:28.080 --> 00:55:29.580
and we're actually doing some work

00:55:29.580 --> 00:55:32.520
with the regional medical
directors on current performance

00:55:32.520 --> 00:55:33.780
of age step care processes.

00:55:33.780 --> 00:55:37.500
So I would hope that we would
start to see that pick up,

00:55:37.500 --> 00:55:38.850
but there will be a lag.

00:55:38.850 --> 00:55:40.620
- [ Penny] Okay, hope so. Meghana?

00:55:40.620 --> 00:55:42.210
- Thank you, just about your question

00:55:42.210 --> 00:55:44.040
on the mortality rates.

00:55:44.040 --> 00:55:46.530
So the summarised
hospital mortality index,

00:55:46.530 --> 00:55:48.900
which looks at deaths within hospital

00:55:48.900 --> 00:55:52.290
and also deaths 30 days after discharge.

00:55:52.290 --> 00:55:55.320
So the organisations in this report

00:55:55.320 --> 00:55:57.450
I have discussed with the
regional medical directors

00:55:57.450 --> 00:56:00.180
and they assure me that all these trusts

00:56:00.180 --> 00:56:03.540
have robust mortality
governance processes.

00:56:03.540 --> 00:56:06.360
They have looked at
the specific conditions

00:56:06.360 --> 00:56:09.810
that have led to the rise in SHMI,

00:56:09.810 --> 00:56:12.000
Summary Hospital Mrtality Index.

00:56:12.000 --> 00:56:15.090
And where necessary they have undertaken

00:56:15.090 --> 00:56:19.440
quality improvement work, such
as introducing care bundles

00:56:19.440 --> 00:56:21.630
say for pneumonia care.

00:56:21.630 --> 00:56:24.660
They have also where necessary

00:56:24.660 --> 00:56:26.700
if the structured judgement reviews,

00:56:26.700 --> 00:56:29.790
which are undertaken for
all deaths in hospitals,

00:56:29.790 --> 00:56:33.150
where they've found there
would be a likely reason

00:56:33.150 --> 00:56:34.620
that this could have been avoidable,

00:56:34.620 --> 00:56:37.800
they have gone on to
investigate it further

00:56:37.800 --> 00:56:39.660
as a patient safety incident.

00:56:39.660 --> 00:56:43.650
And there are some hospitals
where there are issues

00:56:43.650 --> 00:56:46.830
with regard to the change in the data set

00:56:46.830 --> 00:56:51.830
because the same day emergency care data

00:56:52.020 --> 00:56:54.280
is no longer included in the denominator

00:56:55.260 --> 00:57:00.260
and that is not currently
the situation for all trusts.

00:57:01.230 --> 00:57:03.270
So we need to look at
that as well separately.

00:57:03.270 --> 00:57:05.010
- Okay. Thank you. Very helpful.

00:57:05.010 --> 00:57:06.900
So David, just coming back to you,

00:57:06.900 --> 00:57:08.850
I think there were questions
both about the what,

00:57:08.850 --> 00:57:11.416
what are we measuring and, you know,

00:57:11.416 --> 00:57:12.600
this is work in progress

00:57:12.600 --> 00:57:14.070
and I think there's
still some hints on that.

00:57:14.070 --> 00:57:17.580
But the big one that's come up
actually is around variation

00:57:17.580 --> 00:57:20.130
and are we actually showing
enough in these pages

00:57:20.130 --> 00:57:21.210
on the variation

00:57:21.210 --> 00:57:23.850
and then how are we
tackling that variation?

00:57:23.850 --> 00:57:28.020
- No, absolutely. So I'll
take the variation theme away

00:57:28.020 --> 00:57:28.853
with the team.

00:57:28.853 --> 00:57:30.090
Sarah-Jane might want to come on

00:57:30.090 --> 00:57:33.513
particularly on the UEC side of variation.

00:57:34.560 --> 00:57:36.600
And also just a couple
of other quick responses.

00:57:36.600 --> 00:57:40.380
So I'll think with healthy
life expectancy to Sam's point,

00:57:40.380 --> 00:57:44.133
we see, for example,
cancer set by deprivation.

00:57:45.270 --> 00:57:48.120
So there must be other
ways in which we can bring

00:57:48.120 --> 00:57:50.280
that data together, at
least the bits that we have

00:57:50.280 --> 00:57:52.590
because I think it's a
really important challenge

00:57:52.590 --> 00:57:53.423
for us all.

00:57:54.780 --> 00:57:57.150
And then just to Louise's
point around communication,

00:57:57.150 --> 00:58:00.360
again, whether it's maternity,
inpatient or cancer,

00:58:00.360 --> 00:58:02.850
communication surveys
are really live and real

00:58:02.850 --> 00:58:05.190
and we can see that on a pathway basis.

00:58:05.190 --> 00:58:08.040
So how might we bring subsets
of that if not the holistic,

00:58:08.040 --> 00:58:11.910
until the new patient
experience lead comes in.

00:58:11.910 --> 00:58:14.340
But maybe Sarah-Jane, did you
want to pick up Ravi's point

00:58:14.340 --> 00:58:17.700
around variation and the
granulation of the data

00:58:17.700 --> 00:58:19.203
on 12 hour waits?

00:58:19.203 --> 00:58:21.720
- Absolutely, so on
this issue of stability

00:58:21.720 --> 00:58:25.050
versus is it always the same people?

00:58:25.050 --> 00:58:29.160
Broadly, yes, although we do
see some really heroic leaps

00:58:29.160 --> 00:58:33.420
out of the bottom group and
we've actually seen this year,

00:58:33.420 --> 00:58:36.240
some people go from the bottom
to almost the top performing

00:58:36.240 --> 00:58:37.560
in a single year

00:58:37.560 --> 00:58:40.233
because they've really
driven the transformation.

00:58:41.430 --> 00:58:44.910
We do know why in all of the organisations

00:58:44.910 --> 00:58:47.730
that pull performing on the metrics,

00:58:47.730 --> 00:58:51.660
it's always a combination of
systems and process issues

00:58:51.660 --> 00:58:53.820
coupled with culture and leadership

00:58:53.820 --> 00:58:57.120
that's not tackling them
in the way that's needed.

00:58:57.120 --> 00:59:00.240
Our interventions help and
support to look at both.

00:59:00.240 --> 00:59:02.100
I think in terms of what we do,

00:59:02.100 --> 00:59:05.880
we've got a time limited offer
principally led by GIRFT,

00:59:05.880 --> 00:59:07.620
the Getting It Right First Time team

00:59:07.620 --> 00:59:10.530
that exists to drive
out clinical variation,

00:59:10.530 --> 00:59:13.650
but we're increasingly
then aligning the metrics

00:59:13.650 --> 00:59:16.410
that they are looking at and
looking at the improvement

00:59:16.410 --> 00:59:18.780
with the national oversight framework.

00:59:18.780 --> 00:59:21.870
So we're really clear about
the way that we are rating

00:59:21.870 --> 00:59:24.510
and ranking trust and
our expectations on board

00:59:24.510 --> 00:59:26.340
for improvement.

00:59:26.340 --> 00:59:28.410
We have actually, if you
take the ambulance one

00:59:28.410 --> 00:59:31.650
as an example, massively narrowed the gap.

00:59:31.650 --> 00:59:33.990
So we used to have a much bigger variation

00:59:33.990 --> 00:59:36.930
between our best and our poor performers,

00:59:36.930 --> 00:59:39.270
and we've concentrated a lot of our energy

00:59:39.270 --> 00:59:43.980
on getting the tail to get
closer to the middle group,

00:59:43.980 --> 00:59:46.500
and that has worked quite
well in ambulance services.

00:59:46.500 --> 00:59:48.360
We've got a way to go
I think on things like

00:59:48.360 --> 00:59:51.600
four and 12 hours, but it's
an important balance I think,

00:59:51.600 --> 00:59:53.820
and one always for conversation

00:59:53.820 --> 00:59:54.990
about how much you support

00:59:54.990 --> 00:59:58.470
versus how much you hold to account.

00:59:58.470 --> 01:00:01.560
So we're trying to get that
balance of time limited support,

01:00:01.560 --> 01:00:04.380
opportunity, and then
the sort of escalation

01:00:04.380 --> 01:00:06.123
by the accountability framework.

01:00:07.470 --> 01:00:09.420
- Thanks Sarah-Jane, and
there was one final point

01:00:09.420 --> 01:00:13.220
just around MMR and learning
from international comparators.

01:00:13.220 --> 01:00:15.139
I don't know whether Amanda?

01:00:15.139 --> 01:00:16.320
- Yeah, just before that though David,

01:00:16.320 --> 01:00:18.690
so it's a bit about showing the variation

01:00:18.690 --> 01:00:20.850
in performance across,

01:00:20.850 --> 01:00:23.460
as we were just talking
about urgent emergency care.

01:00:23.460 --> 01:00:26.310
But I think there's also
variation in performance

01:00:26.310 --> 01:00:27.930
across the quality metrics.

01:00:27.930 --> 01:00:31.140
So, I'm always very
conscious that that average,

01:00:31.140 --> 01:00:32.670
whether it's for hypertension management,

01:00:32.670 --> 01:00:35.640
cholesterol management,
diabetes management,

01:00:35.640 --> 01:00:40.200
the average belies a very
significant variation underneath.

01:00:40.200 --> 01:00:41.910
So I think if we could see some of that,

01:00:41.910 --> 01:00:43.770
the scale of variation.

01:00:43.770 --> 01:00:45.540
And then similarly have this conversation,

01:00:45.540 --> 01:00:48.990
what are we doing with the
people or the organisations

01:00:48.990 --> 01:00:51.750
that are really under delivering?

01:00:51.750 --> 01:00:52.710
- [David] We'll look into that.

01:00:52.710 --> 01:00:54.720
- [Penny] Yeah.
- Absolutely.

01:00:54.720 --> 01:00:56.130
- [Penny] Did you want to mention MMR?

01:00:56.130 --> 01:00:59.280
- So we're still off where we need to be

01:00:59.280 --> 01:01:01.590
around MMR delivery to children.

01:01:01.590 --> 01:01:04.830
We have made some changes
in the GP contract

01:01:04.830 --> 01:01:08.747
that come onto force next
week to particularly support

01:01:10.410 --> 01:01:14.310
general practise in areas where
it's very, very challenging

01:01:14.310 --> 01:01:18.750
to deliver the coverage
that we need to see.

01:01:18.750 --> 01:01:23.343
So some extra incentive to
shift the dial up a bit.

01:01:24.450 --> 01:01:26.610
International comparison's difficult

01:01:26.610 --> 01:01:28.470
where you see there are some places

01:01:28.470 --> 01:01:31.980
where there are sort of rules around

01:01:31.980 --> 01:01:33.933
school entry for example.

01:01:35.460 --> 01:01:38.973
But this is a challenge
not just in this country.

01:01:40.650 --> 01:01:42.390
- Okay. Thank you.

01:01:42.390 --> 01:01:44.850
Elizabeth, did you want
to come back on finance

01:01:44.850 --> 01:01:46.590
over and above what Andrew said?

01:01:46.590 --> 01:01:48.050
- A couple of brief points, if I may.

01:01:48.050 --> 01:01:49.530
So thank you. Job's done.

01:01:49.530 --> 01:01:51.330
Thank you, Andrew. That's excellent.

01:01:52.230 --> 01:01:55.950
So we are the reports at month 10

01:01:55.950 --> 01:01:57.900
we have actually got
month 11 numbers in now,

01:01:57.900 --> 01:01:59.760
so they're tracking.

01:01:59.760 --> 01:02:04.760
So we move from a small overspend
in aggregate of 71 million

01:02:04.920 --> 01:02:08.790
to a small surplus at this
point in the year, 30 million.

01:02:08.790 --> 01:02:09.660
So we're on track.

01:02:09.660 --> 01:02:13.620
We're definitely not complacent as an NHS

01:02:13.620 --> 01:02:15.243
not just a finance community.

01:02:16.320 --> 01:02:18.600
You always hold your nerve
at this time of the year

01:02:18.600 --> 01:02:20.100
as we're working through things.

01:02:20.100 --> 01:02:22.530
But just to let members know,

01:02:22.530 --> 01:02:24.720
we also did a month nine exercise

01:02:24.720 --> 01:02:27.060
that we reported through ARAC,

01:02:27.060 --> 01:02:30.300
which is effectively a dry
run for the year end accounts.

01:02:30.300 --> 01:02:32.190
So we did that at month nine.

01:02:32.190 --> 01:02:34.290
And any issues that we have identified,

01:02:34.290 --> 01:02:36.120
we've effectively then
worked through those

01:02:36.120 --> 01:02:37.290
in advance the year end.

01:02:37.290 --> 01:02:41.250
So I'm expecting a smoother
audit and a smoother process,

01:02:41.250 --> 01:02:43.980
which effectively means we're
hoping to bring our timetable

01:02:43.980 --> 01:02:47.130
forward in terms of laying our accounts

01:02:47.130 --> 01:02:49.290
before parliament, et cetera.

01:02:49.290 --> 01:02:53.223
Since COVID, they have been
delayed quite significantly,

01:02:54.169 --> 01:02:55.950
so we're pulling that back.

01:02:55.950 --> 01:02:58.650
The numbers are in the
report, so from a perspective

01:02:58.650 --> 01:03:03.650
of system performance is a
429 million pound deficit.

01:03:04.080 --> 01:03:08.010
15 systems are off plan at currently.

01:03:08.010 --> 01:03:09.810
I guess the thing I would want to pull out

01:03:09.810 --> 01:03:12.990
that just to elaborate
on what Andrew described

01:03:12.990 --> 01:03:16.740
was of the 14 systems, we've now also got

01:03:16.740 --> 01:03:20.970
board assurance statements
from each of these systems.

01:03:20.970 --> 01:03:23.280
Boards have been fully briefed.

01:03:23.280 --> 01:03:26.070
We've asked for recovery
actions for each of these boards

01:03:26.070 --> 01:03:30.150
to go through and be
discussed in board meetings.

01:03:30.150 --> 01:03:33.510
And we've also provided information

01:03:33.510 --> 01:03:36.450
that looks at variation
for each of these boards

01:03:36.450 --> 01:03:41.450
to have a real focus on, I guess.

01:03:41.550 --> 01:03:43.260
And so it comes back to the point about

01:03:43.260 --> 01:03:46.530
how are we providing
information around variation

01:03:46.530 --> 01:03:50.070
at a board level that
enables actionable insight.

01:03:50.070 --> 01:03:51.747
So that's the approach
that we've taken this year

01:03:51.747 --> 01:03:53.343
and we continue to take.

01:03:54.600 --> 01:03:56.790
The other thing that we're doing is,

01:03:56.790 --> 01:03:58.920
I don't know how others have felt,

01:03:58.920 --> 01:04:00.480
but sometimes at this time of the year,

01:04:00.480 --> 01:04:03.810
as Jim's described, we
pivot to the new year

01:04:03.810 --> 01:04:06.450
and it's almost like this year is done.

01:04:06.450 --> 01:04:09.690
So we are absolutely
working with those teams

01:04:09.690 --> 01:04:10.830
that have gone off track

01:04:10.830 --> 01:04:12.960
to make sure the run
rate doesn't deteriorate.

01:04:12.960 --> 01:04:17.340
So we have to keep at pace
as we go into next year.

01:04:17.340 --> 01:04:20.940
And then from a rewards
and incentives perspective,

01:04:20.940 --> 01:04:23.820
we've also tracked back
because I know the board

01:04:23.820 --> 01:04:28.410
have challenged me about the
withholding of DSF for example.

01:04:28.410 --> 01:04:31.092
And the residual DSF that we-

01:04:31.092 --> 01:04:31.925
- [Board Member] DSF?

01:04:31.925 --> 01:04:34.173
- Yes sorry, sorry, Bren.

01:04:35.700 --> 01:04:37.980
Deficit Support Funding.

01:04:37.980 --> 01:04:39.600
Essentially what we've done

01:04:39.600 --> 01:04:43.800
is the deficit support funding
is that had to be earned

01:04:43.800 --> 01:04:46.230
during the year, and our
principle hasn't changed.

01:04:46.230 --> 01:04:48.030
This is a rules-based system.

01:04:48.030 --> 01:04:50.730
We are redistributing
that to those providers

01:04:50.730 --> 01:04:52.620
and systems that have delivered,

01:04:52.620 --> 01:04:56.190
but we've also recognised
that in a number of places,

01:04:56.190 --> 01:04:59.160
a provider might be in a
system where they've delivered,

01:04:59.160 --> 01:05:01.050
but the system hasn't delivered itself,

01:05:01.050 --> 01:05:03.330
so we are recognising
that good performance.

01:05:03.330 --> 01:05:07.530
So we're trying to do that
in a very level-handed way.

01:05:07.530 --> 01:05:10.710
And then from a capital position,
when you read the capital,

01:05:10.710 --> 01:05:13.800
please don't get distressed
about the level of underspend

01:05:13.800 --> 01:05:15.450
at this time of year.

01:05:15.450 --> 01:05:17.460
I've got good assurance now.

01:05:17.460 --> 01:05:19.683
We did an exercise at month nine.

01:05:20.700 --> 01:05:25.320
I think this underspend
currently is a consequence

01:05:25.320 --> 01:05:28.320
of the fact that we've been
doing annual capital planning

01:05:28.320 --> 01:05:31.443
and we're just about to pivot
to four year allocations.

01:05:32.280 --> 01:05:36.330
So I have on good authority
now with a lot of work

01:05:36.330 --> 01:05:38.610
that we will probably
underspend our capital budget

01:05:38.610 --> 01:05:41.970
by 39 million in aggregate
across the group.

01:05:41.970 --> 01:05:45.303
It's 14 billion in total, so
that's quite tight management.

01:05:48.884 --> 01:05:51.300
So I would say incredibly well
done to everybody out there.

01:05:51.300 --> 01:05:54.330
Let's not overspend our
capital in the last quarter.

01:05:54.330 --> 01:05:56.493
I actually remember if on
the monitor days actually

01:05:56.493 --> 01:05:58.410
that this used to happen.

01:05:58.410 --> 01:05:59.970
And then just finally, if I may,

01:05:59.970 --> 01:06:02.730
a massive shout out to
the finance community.

01:06:02.730 --> 01:06:07.730
So this year, in October, we
introduced a moved to ISFE2.

01:06:10.170 --> 01:06:11.280
I'm really pleased to say-

01:06:11.280 --> 01:06:12.363
- [Penny] What's that mean?

01:06:12.363 --> 01:06:16.350
- This is the integrated finance system-

01:06:16.350 --> 01:06:17.363
- [Penny] Oh, that's right.

01:06:18.615 --> 01:06:22.597
- That NHS England, all ICBs and CSUs use.

01:06:24.480 --> 01:06:26.700
- You're losing money
by the pound Elizabeth

01:06:26.700 --> 01:06:28.650
going in my pot for three letter acronyms

01:06:28.650 --> 01:06:30.720
- Sorry, sorry, sorry.

01:06:30.720 --> 01:06:32.670
- [Penny] I'm going to
have a charging system.

01:06:32.670 --> 01:06:34.890
- I'm going to say sorry, Bren,

01:06:34.890 --> 01:06:36.780
every time it happens.

01:06:36.780 --> 01:06:39.150
But a massive shout out to the team.

01:06:39.150 --> 01:06:42.810
So this was a massive exercise.

01:06:42.810 --> 01:06:44.910
I'm pleased to say we've
just come out of hyper care

01:06:44.910 --> 01:06:47.100
so things are working well.

01:06:47.100 --> 01:06:49.260
And then the other thing
that probably went unnoticed

01:06:49.260 --> 01:06:52.800
to the board was we have a
financial reporting system

01:06:52.800 --> 01:06:54.780
that provides all of this information,

01:06:54.780 --> 01:06:56.583
that collects all the planning data.

01:06:57.780 --> 01:07:00.810
In the middle of collecting various plans

01:07:00.810 --> 01:07:03.090
and monthly reportings,
we've actually moved

01:07:03.090 --> 01:07:06.420
to a new financial reporting system.

01:07:06.420 --> 01:07:09.030
I wouldn't say it's gone
without any glitches,

01:07:09.030 --> 01:07:11.400
but I'm pleased to say
that we navigated that

01:07:11.400 --> 01:07:14.580
in a six week window
after considerable efforts

01:07:14.580 --> 01:07:15.540
by all of the teams.

01:07:15.540 --> 01:07:19.130
So we now feel from a
cyber risk perspective,

01:07:19.130 --> 01:07:20.250
we're in a much better place.

01:07:20.250 --> 01:07:24.180
So lots of things going on
behind the scenes to ensure

01:07:24.180 --> 01:07:26.843
that the quality of the financial
information coming through

01:07:26.843 --> 01:07:28.833
to you, we can have confidence in.

01:07:29.790 --> 01:07:32.670
- Okay, great. Thank you. Very helpful.

01:07:32.670 --> 01:07:35.640
I was a bit remiss in
the last item, David,

01:07:35.640 --> 01:07:37.770
that Andrew made the
point about having more

01:07:37.770 --> 01:07:41.820
productivity metrics in
the performance report,

01:07:41.820 --> 01:07:42.960
which I think would be good to do

01:07:42.960 --> 01:07:44.700
and then we could ideally see how it links

01:07:44.700 --> 01:07:46.950
then to overall financial performance.

01:07:46.950 --> 01:07:48.960
- Yeah, I know Penny, you've asked Andy

01:07:48.960 --> 01:07:49.950
to come to the next...

01:07:49.950 --> 01:07:52.080
Andy to come to the next meeting.

01:07:52.080 --> 01:07:54.660
So we do provide very, very detailed

01:07:54.660 --> 01:07:57.420
monthly productivity
packs to all the providers

01:07:57.420 --> 01:07:58.950
which are available to all the board

01:07:58.950 --> 01:08:01.050
and I believe that Karen Smith

01:08:01.050 --> 01:08:03.350
is actually sending
them out to MPs as well.

01:08:03.350 --> 01:08:05.070
- Oh, interesting.

01:08:05.070 --> 01:08:06.633
Okay, yep, Sam?

01:08:08.610 --> 01:08:11.250
- Yeah, rightly a lot
of focus on the acute.

01:08:11.250 --> 01:08:13.380
I suppose I'm fast
forwarding a year's time

01:08:13.380 --> 01:08:14.760
and asking the question,

01:08:14.760 --> 01:08:19.760
how will we see financially
the evidence of the left shift,

01:08:20.190 --> 01:08:25.190
neighbourhoods, digital,
and the prevention

01:08:25.200 --> 01:08:28.367
that's within our gift in the NHS?

01:08:28.367 --> 01:08:31.650
- [Penny] Yeah, Elizbeth,
you want to take that?

01:08:31.650 --> 01:08:33.850
- So I'm going to give you an answer

01:08:35.310 --> 01:08:38.298
that's not just from a finance person.

01:08:38.298 --> 01:08:40.230
So I have expressed Penny.

01:08:40.230 --> 01:08:43.560
I'm a bit frustrated in
that everyone's asking

01:08:43.560 --> 01:08:47.250
for the evidence of left
shift to be through the money

01:08:47.250 --> 01:08:49.830
and we will see that and
we're already seeing it

01:08:49.830 --> 01:08:51.780
when we talk about planning later today,

01:08:51.780 --> 01:08:53.700
you'll start to see that.

01:08:53.700 --> 01:08:57.270
But I also think we need
to measure the outcomes

01:08:57.270 --> 01:09:02.270
and not get into the trap of good outcomes

01:09:02.820 --> 01:09:04.350
is just because we're spending more money.

01:09:04.350 --> 01:09:08.220
So what I'm looking for
I think is that we spend

01:09:08.220 --> 01:09:11.250
money effectively across
all parts of the sector

01:09:11.250 --> 01:09:14.850
to drive improved outcomes and to improve

01:09:14.850 --> 01:09:17.160
our mortality metrics or whatever else

01:09:17.160 --> 01:09:18.510
that we're talking about.

01:09:18.510 --> 01:09:21.570
So, I agree that there will be some things

01:09:21.570 --> 01:09:23.880
we will naturally want
to pay attention to,

01:09:23.880 --> 01:09:25.653
but it mustn't be the only thing.

01:09:26.520 --> 01:09:28.497
- Yeah, I think there maybe

01:09:28.497 --> 01:09:30.567
and I think let's not get into it now

01:09:30.567 --> 01:09:31.890
because it could be quite
a long conversation,

01:09:31.890 --> 01:09:36.890
but I think there's a
piece about how we look at,

01:09:37.200 --> 01:09:40.980
which would go below the
system level financial data

01:09:40.980 --> 01:09:44.370
to say how are the different
bits within any system working?

01:09:44.370 --> 01:09:47.070
'Cause I think at some
point it goes systems

01:09:47.070 --> 01:09:48.660
and then provider trust.

01:09:48.660 --> 01:09:50.523
It doesn't have, for example,

01:09:52.620 --> 01:09:56.010
how do we think our performance is looking

01:09:56.010 --> 01:09:57.210
across GP practises?

01:09:57.210 --> 01:09:58.110
We have some measures,

01:09:58.110 --> 01:10:00.600
so I think there is
something we could look at

01:10:00.600 --> 01:10:04.710
around all of this and then
how are we going to assess

01:10:04.710 --> 01:10:06.240
neighbourhood provision.

01:10:06.240 --> 01:10:08.490
Is it working efficiently and effectively?

01:10:08.490 --> 01:10:11.910
- Yeah, and we have also obviously
the payment policy groups

01:10:11.910 --> 01:10:13.200
as well that's looking at that.

01:10:13.200 --> 01:10:14.520
Sorry if that sounded defensive,

01:10:14.520 --> 01:10:17.730
it's just I keep being
asked the same question

01:10:17.730 --> 01:10:19.320
and from my previous role,

01:10:19.320 --> 01:10:21.840
I'm really keen to see the outcomes

01:10:21.840 --> 01:10:24.690
and that we're spending every
penny more productively,

01:10:24.690 --> 01:10:25.920
more wisely, more efficiently.

01:10:25.920 --> 01:10:27.270
- Which will be the
healthcare value equation.

01:10:27.270 --> 01:10:28.740
Right, I've got a few hands up now

01:10:28.740 --> 01:10:29.970
and we're slightly limited for time,

01:10:29.970 --> 01:10:30.930
so I'm going to be quite focused.

01:10:30.930 --> 01:10:35.930
So I've got Glen, I've got
Andrew and I've got Paul.

01:10:36.363 --> 01:10:37.500
I'll just take those three.

01:10:37.500 --> 01:10:38.880
- Yeah. I'll be quick chair.

01:10:38.880 --> 01:10:42.450
So I mean, just that's
not an easy thing to track

01:10:42.450 --> 01:10:45.300
would be one of my main answers on that.

01:10:45.300 --> 01:10:49.140
Even in an acute setting,
this increasingly

01:10:49.140 --> 01:10:51.300
a lot of our clinical staff are doing work

01:10:51.300 --> 01:10:52.620
that is about left shift.

01:10:52.620 --> 01:10:55.680
So if you take elderly care
physicians for example,

01:10:55.680 --> 01:10:57.870
they're spending a lot of
time actually outreaching

01:10:57.870 --> 01:11:01.590
into primary care, also
advice and guidance.

01:11:01.590 --> 01:11:04.620
So we will see a lot of
left shift taking place,

01:11:04.620 --> 01:11:07.520
but it could still appear to
be spent in an acute setting.

01:11:09.060 --> 01:11:09.893
- [Penny] Andrew?

01:11:09.893 --> 01:11:13.113
- So my point's on deficit
support for next year.

01:11:13.950 --> 01:11:16.015
So is it shrinking, Elizabeth?

01:11:16.015 --> 01:11:17.145
- [Elizabeth] Yes.

01:11:17.145 --> 01:11:19.620
- And do you feel we've
got the right incentives?

01:11:19.620 --> 01:11:24.150
Because where we've withdrawn
money, I'm slightly worried

01:11:24.150 --> 01:11:27.840
that people give up given that the quantum

01:11:27.840 --> 01:11:29.820
is so big in some, you know,

01:11:29.820 --> 01:11:31.920
some of the bigger systems here.

01:11:31.920 --> 01:11:34.827
So it's just a reflection really around

01:11:34.827 --> 01:11:38.130
are we pitching this right and creating

01:11:38.130 --> 01:11:39.750
the right kind of motivation here

01:11:39.750 --> 01:11:42.270
to get into a balanced position?

01:11:42.270 --> 01:11:43.680
- [Penny] Do You want to take that one?

01:11:43.680 --> 01:11:44.513
- And then we'll have Paul's question?

01:11:44.513 --> 01:11:46.800
- Yeah, sure, so on the
deficit support funding

01:11:46.800 --> 01:11:48.900
as part of the 10 year health plan,

01:11:48.900 --> 01:11:52.080
we agreed that we would
withdraw it at a pace

01:11:52.080 --> 01:11:53.880
that would take us about three years.

01:11:53.880 --> 01:11:57.000
So that's the rate that
we're taking there.

01:11:57.000 --> 01:11:59.520
For some and obviously there will be some

01:11:59.520 --> 01:12:02.070
challenge providers or systems.

01:12:02.070 --> 01:12:04.890
I think that will be three to five years,

01:12:04.890 --> 01:12:06.870
so we might need to course correct.

01:12:06.870 --> 01:12:10.650
I absolutely agree though,
we've got to give people

01:12:10.650 --> 01:12:12.690
a reasonable chance to get to break even

01:12:12.690 --> 01:12:14.730
otherwise people will naturally give up.

01:12:14.730 --> 01:12:18.240
People will push to get
to break even those .

01:12:18.240 --> 01:12:19.500
But in all of the instances

01:12:19.500 --> 01:12:21.690
where we've withdrawn
deficit support funding

01:12:21.690 --> 01:12:25.440
in parallel we're tracking
that there are opportunities

01:12:25.440 --> 01:12:28.710
to make change at the pace that
the deficit support funding

01:12:28.710 --> 01:12:30.102
is coming out.

01:12:30.102 --> 01:12:31.807
- [Andrew] Okay, that's fair.

01:12:31.807 --> 01:12:32.880
- [Penny] Paul?

01:12:32.880 --> 01:12:37.250
- Just linking this
discussion about the success

01:12:37.250 --> 01:12:40.020
of this year with the money,
the previous discussion

01:12:40.020 --> 01:12:42.843
around variation and
the variation obviously

01:12:42.843 --> 01:12:44.943
with the money this year has diminished.

01:12:47.911 --> 01:12:51.050
And so there is a series of experiences

01:12:52.980 --> 01:12:57.840
and Andrew went through
the list of the ICBs

01:12:57.840 --> 01:13:02.840
that had successfully come to
sort of deficits this year.

01:13:04.110 --> 01:13:05.970
I think at the beginning of the year,

01:13:05.970 --> 01:13:08.910
they wouldn't have
believed that was possible.

01:13:08.910 --> 01:13:11.880
So around the country, there are people

01:13:11.880 --> 01:13:14.430
that have done things with your support

01:13:14.430 --> 01:13:16.500
that they didn't believe is possible.

01:13:16.500 --> 01:13:19.950
This year, we are facing
people wanting to do things

01:13:19.950 --> 01:13:22.050
in a whole range of areas not money,

01:13:22.050 --> 01:13:23.910
that they don't think is possible.

01:13:23.910 --> 01:13:27.120
I think those people that
have done something now

01:13:27.120 --> 01:13:30.780
because of your help,
we should think about,

01:13:30.780 --> 01:13:34.800
we should ask them, what
is it that's happened?

01:13:34.800 --> 01:13:36.867
Because they are now doing
something they didn't believe

01:13:36.867 --> 01:13:38.730
was possible and next year

01:13:38.730 --> 01:13:40.800
we're going to want a lot
of people to do things

01:13:40.800 --> 01:13:42.846
they don't believe are possible today.

01:13:42.846 --> 01:13:46.710
- Yeah, so I don't know if
Jeremy or Mark want to comment,

01:13:46.710 --> 01:13:49.650
but we had a CFO event and I know Jeremy

01:13:49.650 --> 01:13:51.570
was able to make the whole day as-

01:13:51.570 --> 01:13:52.403
- [Penny] Fiance Officer.

01:13:52.403 --> 01:13:53.763
- Chief Finance Officer.

01:13:56.820 --> 01:13:59.463
Sorry, yes. One of those events.

01:14:00.390 --> 01:14:05.390
And we picked individuals
and systems and providers

01:14:06.450 --> 01:14:09.840
across the country that had
made the biggest step change,

01:14:09.840 --> 01:14:11.190
and they still weren't perfect,

01:14:11.190 --> 01:14:12.930
but you could see this rate of improvement

01:14:12.930 --> 01:14:14.580
that was really stepped.

01:14:14.580 --> 01:14:18.570
And what was interesting
was I specifically picked

01:14:18.570 --> 01:14:20.223
not the usual suspects.

01:14:21.270 --> 01:14:24.690
And it was fantastic, really, really good.

01:14:24.690 --> 01:14:26.790
And I think I've had the
most amount of response

01:14:26.790 --> 01:14:30.510
from a CFO event because it
wasn't the usual suspects,

01:14:30.510 --> 01:14:33.090
don't get me wrong, we rely
on our usual suspects as well,

01:14:33.090 --> 01:14:35.970
and they're doing a fantastic job.

01:14:35.970 --> 01:14:39.420
But where you have providers,
I won't name them but,

01:14:39.420 --> 01:14:41.280
but providers and individuals

01:14:41.280 --> 01:14:43.680
that have moved their agency budgets

01:14:43.680 --> 01:14:47.250
that were 10 million pounds a year

01:14:47.250 --> 01:14:49.533
to 400,000 pounds a year.

01:14:50.700 --> 01:14:54.060
And their staff survey
results have been maintained.

01:14:54.060 --> 01:14:57.030
They didn't improve, because I
tried to triangulate as well,

01:14:57.030 --> 01:14:59.550
and staff and patient experience on this,

01:14:59.550 --> 01:15:01.470
then I think that was
something and a learning

01:15:01.470 --> 01:15:02.303
for everyone else.

01:15:02.303 --> 01:15:03.136
And that individual,

01:15:03.136 --> 01:15:04.890
not only did they get the kudos actually

01:15:04.890 --> 01:15:08.640
of they were probably somebody
that people didn't know,

01:15:08.640 --> 01:15:10.500
everyone's gone to see, how did you do it?

01:15:10.500 --> 01:15:12.180
And I think the more we can do that,

01:15:12.180 --> 01:15:13.773
I think is incredible.

01:15:14.730 --> 01:15:15.840
Yeah.

01:15:15.840 --> 01:15:19.440
- My observation would be,
the thing at the conference,

01:15:19.440 --> 01:15:20.280
a couple of things.

01:15:20.280 --> 01:15:24.360
First of all, the financial
metrics weren't delivered

01:15:24.360 --> 01:15:28.950
at the cost of other
metrics, so both can be done,

01:15:28.950 --> 01:15:31.200
and I think one of the recurring themes

01:15:31.200 --> 01:15:33.630
was how the finance directors were working

01:15:33.630 --> 01:15:35.850
with the chief executives
in the way that Elizabeth

01:15:35.850 --> 01:15:39.300
and Jim have worked,
that it's a joint plan.

01:15:39.300 --> 01:15:41.220
So rather than finance happening over here

01:15:41.220 --> 01:15:42.390
and then headcount over here

01:15:42.390 --> 01:15:45.090
and then the patient
delivering somewhere else,

01:15:45.090 --> 01:15:46.380
actually having a joint plan,

01:15:46.380 --> 01:15:48.660
I think the key issue
for this year then is,

01:15:48.660 --> 01:15:50.010
what's the joint agenda?

01:15:50.010 --> 01:15:51.720
It's a pretty tricky year.

01:15:51.720 --> 01:15:54.000
How do Jim and Elizabeth communicate

01:15:54.000 --> 01:15:56.430
that out and then how does
that agenda get shared

01:15:56.430 --> 01:15:58.350
with all the chief execs
and the finance directors

01:15:58.350 --> 01:15:59.940
across the country to deliver that?

01:15:59.940 --> 01:16:02.167
But I think it's two themes for this year

01:16:02.167 --> 01:16:04.667
that have been less
recurring in previous years

01:16:04.667 --> 01:16:09.180
is the FDs delivering their
plan with the chief executives

01:16:09.180 --> 01:16:10.410
and it's been a joint agenda.

01:16:10.410 --> 01:16:15.240
- Yeah. Great. Right. Let's move on.

01:16:15.240 --> 01:16:19.290
So Sarah-Jane, we're coming
on to emergency preparedness,

01:16:19.290 --> 01:16:22.803
resilience and response assurance report.

01:16:24.540 --> 01:16:27.090
I think you should take this as read,

01:16:27.090 --> 01:16:30.720
but are there any specific
things you just want to flag?

01:16:30.720 --> 01:16:32.760
- Just briefly, Penny,
if I may, thank you.

01:16:32.760 --> 01:16:36.720
So it's an increasingly
complex volatile world,

01:16:36.720 --> 01:16:38.190
and we've got new and emerging risks

01:16:38.190 --> 01:16:41.640
that we're having to manage
through with permission

01:16:41.640 --> 01:16:43.230
and I'll have to keep saying EPRR,

01:16:43.230 --> 01:16:44.490
otherwise I'm going to extend the item.

01:16:44.490 --> 01:16:45.570
- [Penny] That one you can have.

01:16:45.570 --> 01:16:47.533
- So we explain what it is.

01:16:47.533 --> 01:16:49.470
Okay, that we need to manage through,

01:16:49.470 --> 01:16:51.690
and that means
strengthening our governance

01:16:51.690 --> 01:16:54.300
and our coordination and
our partnership working

01:16:54.300 --> 01:16:56.580
as both as NHS England
and obviously through down

01:16:56.580 --> 01:16:59.070
into the NHS and the
tripartite arrangement

01:16:59.070 --> 01:17:01.620
that we have with the Department
of Health and Social Care

01:17:01.620 --> 01:17:03.930
and the UK Health Security Agency

01:17:03.930 --> 01:17:07.800
is particularly important in this.

01:17:07.800 --> 01:17:11.850
The most significant risk
that we continue to face

01:17:11.850 --> 01:17:13.770
is a pandemic.

01:17:13.770 --> 01:17:17.168
We've obviously got the
learning from COVID-19 modules

01:17:17.168 --> 01:17:21.450
emergent and we are
fully cognizant of those.

01:17:21.450 --> 01:17:24.270
But we've also undertaken a exercise

01:17:24.270 --> 01:17:25.950
for a different kind of pandemic.

01:17:25.950 --> 01:17:29.820
It was called Exercise Pegasus
at the end of last year,

01:17:29.820 --> 01:17:32.460
and we've been bringing the
learning together from that

01:17:32.460 --> 01:17:36.030
with the learning from COVID
in terms of highlighting

01:17:36.030 --> 01:17:38.610
our areas of vulnerability
moving forwards.

01:17:38.610 --> 01:17:40.350
Beyond that, it is cyber.

01:17:40.350 --> 01:17:43.560
Mark touched on that in his report.

01:17:43.560 --> 01:17:45.930
That's our next most substantial risk,

01:17:45.930 --> 01:17:49.410
and it is increasingly how
long these cyber incident

01:17:49.410 --> 01:17:50.760
lasts for.

01:17:50.760 --> 01:17:52.560
The initial business continuity,

01:17:52.560 --> 01:17:55.740
we often need to expand
out for month periods now,

01:17:55.740 --> 01:17:59.460
and we're also increasingly
having to deal with resilience

01:17:59.460 --> 01:18:01.950
of some of our suppliers
and partner organisations.

01:18:01.950 --> 01:18:04.620
We've got a cyber incident
ongoing at the moment

01:18:04.620 --> 01:18:06.990
related to one of the organisations

01:18:06.990 --> 01:18:09.363
that we procure a lot of supplies from.

01:18:10.800 --> 01:18:15.000
Just then briefly things
that we've managed this year,

01:18:15.000 --> 01:18:17.430
there's a whole range of things
we've listed in the report

01:18:17.430 --> 01:18:21.660
from a 111 outage which went
down on a Saturday morning

01:18:21.660 --> 01:18:23.637
and we had no 111 system for several hours

01:18:23.637 --> 01:18:27.330
and one can only imagine the
chaos that ensued from that.

01:18:27.330 --> 01:18:30.930
We've also supported the
evacuation of children

01:18:30.930 --> 01:18:35.130
from Gaza back to the UK for treatment

01:18:35.130 --> 01:18:37.020
managed several rounds
of industrial action,

01:18:37.020 --> 01:18:38.730
and of course we'll need to do that again,

01:18:38.730 --> 01:18:43.203
and we've got this live
incident with Stryker ongoing.

01:18:44.040 --> 01:18:46.320
And then finally, the paper just sets out

01:18:46.320 --> 01:18:48.360
how we've approached the assurance process

01:18:48.360 --> 01:18:49.590
down through the NHS.

01:18:49.590 --> 01:18:53.370
So this has been run
through all organisations.

01:18:53.370 --> 01:18:54.750
They've had to assess themselves

01:18:54.750 --> 01:18:57.090
against variety of standards.

01:18:57.090 --> 01:18:59.850
That have then been
overseen by the regions

01:18:59.850 --> 01:19:02.520
and where there are any gaps in assurance

01:19:02.520 --> 01:19:04.290
there are action plans
that have been followed up

01:19:04.290 --> 01:19:07.380
at their own board or through
the local resilience forums,

01:19:07.380 --> 01:19:11.010
but we're constantly testing
and pushing the standards

01:19:11.010 --> 01:19:11.843
within that report,

01:19:11.843 --> 01:19:14.040
and that's something that the
board felt very strongly on

01:19:14.040 --> 01:19:16.320
last year, particularly around cyber.

01:19:16.320 --> 01:19:18.180
So we adjusted this year's framework

01:19:18.180 --> 01:19:21.810
to ask the question about
resilience for a six month period,

01:19:21.810 --> 01:19:24.720
whereas previously people had
often answered the question

01:19:24.720 --> 01:19:27.090
about, could you deal
with a 72 hour incident

01:19:27.090 --> 01:19:28.680
by means of example?

01:19:28.680 --> 01:19:30.930
So we're very, very busy in this world,

01:19:30.930 --> 01:19:33.660
and I don't foresee, unfortunately

01:19:33.660 --> 01:19:34.800
that that's going to be different

01:19:34.800 --> 01:19:38.340
as we go into the next 12 months.

01:19:38.340 --> 01:19:39.810
- [Penny] Sam?

01:19:39.810 --> 01:19:43.980
- So I increasingly talk
about the left, left shift.

01:19:43.980 --> 01:19:46.163
So in blood pressure,
helping patients to measure

01:19:46.163 --> 01:19:48.600
their own blood pressure as an example.

01:19:48.600 --> 01:19:49.830
Where I come from in Norway,

01:19:49.830 --> 01:19:54.150
each household has a whole raft of things

01:19:54.150 --> 01:19:58.170
that it collects to create the
resilience in any emergency.

01:19:58.170 --> 01:20:01.950
So my question is, what
are we doing in this item

01:20:01.950 --> 01:20:04.140
in terms of what I would
call the left, left shift

01:20:04.140 --> 01:20:07.500
in terms of supporting
patients to be resilient?

01:20:07.500 --> 01:20:10.400
- Okay, let me just add
one amendment to that,

01:20:10.400 --> 01:20:12.870
or one addition to that,

01:20:12.870 --> 01:20:14.753
a bit the same, but slightly different.

01:20:15.900 --> 01:20:20.670
How do we assure ourselves
that through making sure

01:20:20.670 --> 01:20:24.960
that we have assurance around
continued service delivery

01:20:24.960 --> 01:20:26.820
in these particular areas,

01:20:26.820 --> 01:20:31.820
that we don't end up
inadvertently deteriorating health

01:20:32.070 --> 01:20:34.860
by not focusing on the interventions

01:20:34.860 --> 01:20:37.740
that would improve
overall health outcomes,

01:20:37.740 --> 01:20:40.860
some of which may be around
supporting individuals more,

01:20:40.860 --> 01:20:44.670
some of which may be around
good primary care and so on.

01:20:44.670 --> 01:20:46.650
So that we're keeping
an eye on the long term

01:20:46.650 --> 01:20:48.780
as well as the short term.

01:20:48.780 --> 01:20:51.060
- Yeah. Okay.

01:20:51.060 --> 01:20:51.900
- [Penny] Oh, sorry, Duncan.

01:20:51.900 --> 01:20:53.250
Oh yeah.
- [Duncan] Sorry.

01:20:54.148 --> 01:20:55.019
- [Sarah-Jane] If you can
give me one I could answer

01:20:55.019 --> 01:20:56.853
that'd be great.

01:20:56.853 --> 01:20:58.029
After the previous two.

01:21:02.237 --> 01:21:05.970
- Penny, I was reflecting
Sarah-Jane's comment on,

01:21:05.970 --> 01:21:07.800
you know, we've obviously had module three

01:21:07.800 --> 01:21:12.480
of the COVID inquiry, and
in terms of for this board-

01:21:12.480 --> 01:21:16.170
where we make sure we really
kick the tyres on the actions

01:21:16.170 --> 01:21:17.217
that are coming out of that,

01:21:17.217 --> 01:21:19.830
and because to the last questions point,

01:21:19.830 --> 01:21:22.587
that kind of lessons learn around focus,

01:21:22.587 --> 01:21:24.240
and I just think there's
something about us

01:21:24.240 --> 01:21:25.410
really making sure we have time.

01:21:25.410 --> 01:21:28.050
- I think we need to bring
that back very explicitly

01:21:28.050 --> 01:21:29.229
to a future discussion.

01:21:29.229 --> 01:21:32.010
- And specifically
answering that question,

01:21:32.010 --> 01:21:36.870
is if people and their
carers did not take the tests

01:21:36.870 --> 01:21:39.513
during COVID, the nation
would still have it.

01:21:40.500 --> 01:21:45.500
And so we depended upon
human beings and their carers

01:21:45.660 --> 01:21:48.183
to do that testing.

01:21:48.183 --> 01:21:51.510
And so the resilience of
that is a crucial part

01:21:51.510 --> 01:21:52.343
of the resilience.

01:21:52.343 --> 01:21:54.270
So that is in a sense,
answer the question about

01:21:54.270 --> 01:21:59.270
we can increase primary care
and people's own resilience,

01:21:59.610 --> 01:22:02.673
because it's going to be an
essential part of any pandemic.

01:22:03.980 --> 01:22:05.290
- Paul?

01:22:05.290 --> 01:22:08.400
- Two thins, one just
to note that yesterday

01:22:08.400 --> 01:22:11.100
the government's new
pandemic awareness strategy

01:22:11.100 --> 01:22:12.300
was published.

01:22:12.300 --> 01:22:14.550
So I think it's worth
us being aware of that.

01:22:15.510 --> 01:22:17.190
The second thing is I
wasn't quite sure, Penny,

01:22:17.190 --> 01:22:20.610
if you were suggesting
an implied trade off

01:22:20.610 --> 01:22:21.683
of what we're paying attention to.

01:22:21.683 --> 01:22:24.060
I think my reflection, maybe
building on Paul's point

01:22:24.060 --> 01:22:25.620
is that from the last pandemic,

01:22:25.620 --> 01:22:27.990
had we gone into that pandemic

01:22:27.990 --> 01:22:30.150
with a better underlying
health of the population,

01:22:30.150 --> 01:22:31.290
would have come out a bit a lot better.

01:22:31.290 --> 01:22:34.560
And so I think to from
that perspective at least,

01:22:34.560 --> 01:22:36.240
that those two things should dovetail.

01:22:36.240 --> 01:22:38.733
- Okay, so I think one question is,

01:22:39.570 --> 01:22:41.460
where Duncan started and Paul's gone

01:22:41.460 --> 01:22:43.440
and a little bit of mine,

01:22:43.440 --> 01:22:45.570
let's take to another meeting

01:22:45.570 --> 01:22:47.970
when we look at the COVID inquiry

01:22:47.970 --> 01:22:51.450
and what's that mean and
how do we think about it?

01:22:51.450 --> 01:22:53.910
So let's have that specific agenda item.

01:22:53.910 --> 01:22:56.340
I think for here and now, Sarah-Jane,

01:22:56.340 --> 01:22:59.640
I think Sam recognises,
probably a hard one to answer,

01:22:59.640 --> 01:23:02.040
but, you know, Sam's underlying bit is,

01:23:02.040 --> 01:23:04.620
do we look at wider society preparedness

01:23:04.620 --> 01:23:09.390
for particular emergencies?

01:23:09.390 --> 01:23:11.460
I'm happy that my one would pick up

01:23:11.460 --> 01:23:13.230
through that review
from the COVID inquiry.

01:23:13.230 --> 01:23:15.990
- Sure, so first way to answer it is,

01:23:15.990 --> 01:23:18.600
we've definitely left shifted.

01:23:18.600 --> 01:23:20.010
I'm not sure we've left, left yet,

01:23:20.010 --> 01:23:22.620
but this has definitely been
a year where we've spent

01:23:22.620 --> 01:23:26.070
a lot more time on primary
and community resilience.

01:23:26.070 --> 01:23:30.600
And in one of our big
learnings from exercise Pegasus

01:23:30.600 --> 01:23:34.860
was that there was an ability
to use community response

01:23:34.860 --> 01:23:36.510
a lot more than we were able to do

01:23:36.510 --> 01:23:38.220
because it wasn't scaled and ready to go.

01:23:38.220 --> 01:23:41.427
So we are definitely putting
more energy into thinking about

01:23:41.427 --> 01:23:44.040
the resilience of out of hospital

01:23:44.040 --> 01:23:46.890
and how we build some of
those escalation systems.

01:23:46.890 --> 01:23:48.690
We do though work very closely

01:23:48.690 --> 01:23:50.940
with the tripartite
arrangement, as I mentioned

01:23:50.940 --> 01:23:53.340
with Department of Health and Social Care

01:23:53.340 --> 01:23:56.220
and the Health Security
Agency, but also cabinet office

01:23:56.220 --> 01:23:57.480
and cross government,

01:23:57.480 --> 01:24:00.360
just looking at broader
resilience planning,

01:24:00.360 --> 01:24:02.730
which does take those sorts
of things into account.

01:24:02.730 --> 01:24:04.830
So I sit as part of a group

01:24:04.830 --> 01:24:08.310
that's assessing all of the
wider societal response.

01:24:08.310 --> 01:24:09.810
If we look at all
different sorts of issues,

01:24:09.810 --> 01:24:12.960
not just related to health,
but, you know, other things

01:24:12.960 --> 01:24:14.940
that could have a big impact as well.

01:24:14.940 --> 01:24:18.000
So the partnership working
of this is really important

01:24:18.000 --> 01:24:21.510
and one of the reflections
as we go into new DHSC,

01:24:21.510 --> 01:24:24.480
I think is the sort of clearer

01:24:24.480 --> 01:24:27.750
of what's an absolute
responsibility now of NHS England

01:24:27.750 --> 01:24:30.330
to do in this space as
opposed to other agencies

01:24:30.330 --> 01:24:31.163
across government.

01:24:31.163 --> 01:24:33.000
That's one of the things
that we have to...

01:24:33.000 --> 01:24:34.860
We're holding in balance all the time,

01:24:34.860 --> 01:24:37.680
certainly in the men B, the Meningitis B,

01:24:37.680 --> 01:24:40.230
I was very clear that the
NHS was there to respond

01:24:40.230 --> 01:24:42.660
to the decisions that were made

01:24:42.660 --> 01:24:44.700
in the other parts of the partnership

01:24:44.700 --> 01:24:47.460
and we would take them
away and make them happen.

01:24:47.460 --> 01:24:49.080
So it's just getting that right balance

01:24:49.080 --> 01:24:52.530
between our own the own part of that.

01:24:52.530 --> 01:24:53.550
Then just in terms of,

01:24:53.550 --> 01:24:56.640
there's a few bits on the
tracking as things emerge.

01:24:56.640 --> 01:24:58.560
So we immediately scan them for actions

01:24:58.560 --> 01:25:00.270
that we need to take in the here and now

01:25:00.270 --> 01:25:02.040
if there's particularly high risk things

01:25:02.040 --> 01:25:04.590
that we need to address.

01:25:04.590 --> 01:25:08.940
And then we also sort of put a marker down

01:25:08.940 --> 01:25:11.640
for things where we need
to do a more strategic

01:25:11.640 --> 01:25:12.473
piece of work.

01:25:12.473 --> 01:25:14.490
So there's some really
live things at the moment

01:25:14.490 --> 01:25:18.570
like our accuracy of
the way we report mask,

01:25:18.570 --> 01:25:21.030
you know, people's fit
for a mask for example,

01:25:21.030 --> 01:25:22.560
which is something we need to do better on

01:25:22.560 --> 01:25:24.210
in the here and now.

01:25:24.210 --> 01:25:25.830
There's another thing which is much bigger

01:25:25.830 --> 01:25:28.080
that came out of Pegasus for example,

01:25:28.080 --> 01:25:31.440
where we really could not surge
our intensive care capacity

01:25:31.440 --> 01:25:36.440
for children beyond around 200%
which is still pretty good.

01:25:37.548 --> 01:25:38.880
And there isn't an easy answer to the way

01:25:38.880 --> 01:25:41.580
that we would do that because
if there was an easy answer,

01:25:41.580 --> 01:25:44.790
we'd be operating at a very,
very low utilisation figure

01:25:44.790 --> 01:25:45.930
day in, day out.

01:25:45.930 --> 01:25:48.240
And we need to take some
more strategic decisions

01:25:48.240 --> 01:25:50.160
around whether we're happy to operate

01:25:50.160 --> 01:25:51.480
with that sort of risk or not.

01:25:51.480 --> 01:25:53.520
So there's a transactional response

01:25:53.520 --> 01:25:56.190
and there's that more
sort of strategic response

01:25:56.190 --> 01:26:01.190
and how much risk that we are
collectively happy to carry.

01:26:01.350 --> 01:26:04.110
- Okay. All right, thank you.

01:26:04.110 --> 01:26:08.250
So we've now got a few things
which are for information.

01:26:08.250 --> 01:26:09.630
We won't spend time on those,

01:26:09.630 --> 01:26:12.480
but I would ask colleagues to make sure

01:26:12.480 --> 01:26:14.703
that they have read all of those.

01:26:15.930 --> 01:26:18.810
And the final item for today
is a number of questions

01:26:18.810 --> 01:26:22.110
from the public, so I'm
going to read those out

01:26:22.110 --> 01:26:24.840
and ask colleagues to respond to them.

01:26:24.840 --> 01:26:28.973
So the first one I think
either David, you, or Meghana,

01:26:28.973 --> 01:26:30.183
Meghana you taking it?

01:26:30.183 --> 01:26:31.620
Yeah Okay.

01:26:31.620 --> 01:26:33.420
How does NHS England assure itself

01:26:33.420 --> 01:26:36.240
that its oversight arrangements
can distinguish isolated

01:26:36.240 --> 01:26:40.680
clinical error from potential
executive level concealments

01:26:40.680 --> 01:26:43.980
where trust leadership has
acknowledged systemic complaints

01:26:43.980 --> 01:26:47.580
failures, but denied
duty of candour breaches

01:26:47.580 --> 01:26:49.680
in cases of admitted negligence

01:26:49.680 --> 01:26:52.620
and what routes exist for
patients to escalate concerns

01:26:52.620 --> 01:26:55.770
involving alleged misconduct
by senior trust leaders

01:26:55.770 --> 01:26:57.780
beyond internal processes?

01:26:57.780 --> 01:26:58.860
- Thank you, Chair.

01:26:58.860 --> 01:27:01.500
So concerns about the
conduct of chief executives

01:27:01.500 --> 01:27:03.450
or senior trust members should be raised

01:27:03.450 --> 01:27:06.750
with the chair of that
organisation in the first instance.

01:27:06.750 --> 01:27:10.860
If the recipient of that
response is not happy

01:27:10.860 --> 01:27:13.530
with the response, then
they should escalate that.

01:27:13.530 --> 01:27:17.280
If that's a complaint, then to
the health service ombudsman.

01:27:17.280 --> 01:27:20.640
Concerns about alleged criminal
behaviour should be reported

01:27:20.640 --> 01:27:22.470
to the police whilst concerns about

01:27:22.470 --> 01:27:25.140
potential provider
breaches of Regulation 20,

01:27:25.140 --> 01:27:26.880
which is duty of candour,

01:27:26.880 --> 01:27:29.130
should be raised with the
Care Quality Commission

01:27:29.130 --> 01:27:30.963
who are the appropriate regulator.

01:27:31.890 --> 01:27:34.800
It's not NHS's England's
role to investigate

01:27:34.800 --> 01:27:36.930
individual patients complaints

01:27:36.930 --> 01:27:39.750
nor to generally investigate concerns

01:27:39.750 --> 01:27:43.650
about individual senior trust members,

01:27:43.650 --> 01:27:47.520
but where such concerns are
raised with NHS England,

01:27:47.520 --> 01:27:49.440
NHS England will consider
whether they indicate

01:27:49.440 --> 01:27:51.150
broader governance concerns

01:27:51.150 --> 01:27:53.801
about how the trust is run by the board.

01:27:53.801 --> 01:27:55.710
NHS England can and does use

01:27:55.710 --> 01:27:57.780
proportionate assurance reviews,

01:27:57.780 --> 01:27:59.730
provide a licence oversight,

01:27:59.730 --> 01:28:01.530
fit and proper person scrutiny,

01:28:01.530 --> 01:28:04.860
and where appropriate
independent external reviews

01:28:04.860 --> 01:28:08.460
to understand whether concerns
indicate isolated errors

01:28:08.460 --> 01:28:11.760
or potential leadership level concealment.

01:28:11.760 --> 01:28:13.779
- Okay. Thank you, Meghana.

01:28:13.779 --> 01:28:16.562
And I just wonder, Rob,
this is probably for you.

01:28:16.562 --> 01:28:21.562
Can we make sure that those
processes are clearly set out

01:28:21.660 --> 01:28:25.110
on our website because we have
quite a few questions about

01:28:25.110 --> 01:28:27.840
what is the right process
that people need to go through

01:28:27.840 --> 01:28:30.450
and it would be helpful if that was there.

01:28:30.450 --> 01:28:32.460
- We're just in the process
of tidying the website up,

01:28:32.460 --> 01:28:33.630
so I'll make sure that's in.

01:28:33.630 --> 01:28:34.587
- Okay. Thank you very much.

01:28:34.587 --> 01:28:36.390
- And Penny, linked to that.

01:28:36.390 --> 01:28:38.460
Some clarity around whistleblowing

01:28:38.460 --> 01:28:40.920
and where whistleblowers go,

01:28:40.920 --> 01:28:41.913
would be helpful.

01:28:42.780 --> 01:28:44.460
I think there's some confusion out there.

01:28:44.460 --> 01:28:47.670
So what happens if your
own trust doesn't respond

01:28:47.670 --> 01:28:50.580
positively to an issue?

01:28:50.580 --> 01:28:51.840
What do you do next?

01:28:51.840 --> 01:28:54.183
- Yeah. Okay. That would be very helpful.

01:28:55.350 --> 01:28:58.680
So the next one is for Claire.

01:28:58.680 --> 01:29:01.743
We have received several
questions about prescribing.

01:29:03.031 --> 01:29:03.864
- [Clair] Liothyronine.
- Liothyronine.

01:29:06.812 --> 01:29:08.100
And you can tell us what it's for.

01:29:08.100 --> 01:29:10.410
Mainly focused on two points.

01:29:10.410 --> 01:29:15.360
How will NHS England resolve
the misalignment contradiction

01:29:15.360 --> 01:29:19.470
between our policies and
ensuring that guidance

01:29:19.470 --> 01:29:22.380
is clinically coherent and
consistently implemented

01:29:22.380 --> 01:29:26.730
by all ICBs and what steps
will NHS England take

01:29:26.730 --> 01:29:29.760
to address persistent ICB non-compliance

01:29:29.760 --> 01:29:32.760
and ensure that patients
receive equitable access

01:29:32.760 --> 01:29:34.950
where it is clinically indicated?

01:29:34.950 --> 01:29:35.783
- So thank you, Penny.

01:29:35.783 --> 01:29:37.920
So liothyronine is a medication used

01:29:37.920 --> 01:29:39.450
in some thyroid conditions.

01:29:39.450 --> 01:29:41.880
So NHS England have policy guidance

01:29:41.880 --> 01:29:43.560
and prescribing advice on liothyronine,

01:29:43.560 --> 01:29:46.260
which reflects NICE recommendations

01:29:46.260 --> 01:29:48.390
and the evidence that
we have on liothyronine.

01:29:48.390 --> 01:29:50.700
That guidance recommends that liothyronine

01:29:50.700 --> 01:29:52.590
should not be routinely prescribed

01:29:52.590 --> 01:29:55.080
because it is not clinically
or cost effective,

01:29:55.080 --> 01:29:57.360
but sets out the exceptions

01:29:57.360 --> 01:29:59.190
where it may be an
appropriate consideration

01:29:59.190 --> 01:30:00.720
for prescribers.

01:30:00.720 --> 01:30:03.450
The policy position is kept
under review with clinical leads

01:30:03.450 --> 01:30:06.510
who continue to advise that
the evidence remains unchanged

01:30:06.510 --> 01:30:09.210
and the policy should continue as it is.

01:30:09.210 --> 01:30:12.240
NHS England expects that
commissioners and prescribers

01:30:12.240 --> 01:30:14.550
will act with due regard
to the advice provided

01:30:14.550 --> 01:30:15.840
by NHS England.

01:30:15.840 --> 01:30:17.250
As part of the new operating model,

01:30:17.250 --> 01:30:19.350
regions will have
oversight of commissioner

01:30:19.350 --> 01:30:20.880
and provider performance,

01:30:20.880 --> 01:30:22.890
including access to high quality care

01:30:22.890 --> 01:30:24.993
and reduction in health inequalities.

01:30:25.831 --> 01:30:27.751
- Thank you. Very clear.

01:30:27.751 --> 01:30:31.200
And then for Amanda, doctors
have a professional duty

01:30:31.200 --> 01:30:33.510
under the General Medical Council guidance

01:30:33.510 --> 01:30:35.220
to refer patients to specialist care

01:30:35.220 --> 01:30:37.980
where this is in the
patient's best interests.

01:30:37.980 --> 01:30:41.040
The forthcoming general
practise contract introduces

01:30:41.040 --> 01:30:44.040
additional requirements prior to referral.

01:30:44.040 --> 01:30:46.230
Can the board confirm whether NHS England

01:30:46.230 --> 01:30:48.660
has formally engaged with
the General Medical Council

01:30:48.660 --> 01:30:51.240
on how these contractual
requirements interact

01:30:51.240 --> 01:30:52.980
with that professional duty

01:30:52.980 --> 01:30:55.500
and whether any joint risk assessment

01:30:55.500 --> 01:30:57.990
or patient safety review
has been undertaken

01:30:57.990 --> 01:31:00.870
to ensure GPs are not placed in conflict

01:31:00.870 --> 01:31:04.410
between their contractual
and professional obligations?

01:31:04.410 --> 01:31:08.550
- So GP's clinical decision
to refer is unchanged

01:31:08.550 --> 01:31:09.630
in the new contract,

01:31:09.630 --> 01:31:12.270
which comes into effect
on the 1st of April.

01:31:12.270 --> 01:31:15.870
Nothing in the new contract
changes their responsibility

01:31:15.870 --> 01:31:18.330
to act in their patient's best interest.

01:31:18.330 --> 01:31:22.470
There's no new contractual
barriers to referral.

01:31:22.470 --> 01:31:25.380
GPs can refer exactly as they always have,

01:31:25.380 --> 01:31:28.530
and claims about new
hurdles aren't correct.

01:31:28.530 --> 01:31:31.560
The changes actually
relate to secondary care

01:31:31.560 --> 01:31:33.990
and the way in which
referrals are triaged,

01:31:33.990 --> 01:31:36.993
not to GP obligations to act.

01:31:38.190 --> 01:31:40.650
Expanding advice and guidance simply means

01:31:40.650 --> 01:31:44.850
that patients will get faster
consultant-led decisions

01:31:44.850 --> 01:31:48.030
and clearer next steps rather than putting

01:31:48.030 --> 01:31:51.330
any sort of barrier into the process

01:31:51.330 --> 01:31:53.040
by which they get referred.

01:31:53.040 --> 01:31:55.980
The GMC isn't involved
in contractual matters,

01:31:55.980 --> 01:31:59.850
so no formal engagement has
taken place with the GMC

01:31:59.850 --> 01:32:01.200
and that's because their role

01:32:01.200 --> 01:32:03.420
is as a professional regulator.

01:32:03.420 --> 01:32:06.480
And there is no conflict
between what's in the contract

01:32:06.480 --> 01:32:09.270
and the professional duty of GPs.

01:32:09.270 --> 01:32:11.880
- Thank you, and then Duncan,

01:32:11.880 --> 01:32:13.260
we've received a couple of questions

01:32:13.260 --> 01:32:15.840
related to trusts
operating without compliant

01:32:15.840 --> 01:32:19.500
single sex facilities
and maintaining policies

01:32:19.500 --> 01:32:21.810
that are suggested to be inconsistent

01:32:21.810 --> 01:32:24.180
with the Equality Act of 2010.

01:32:24.180 --> 01:32:29.040
Citing tribunal findings,
requests for policy review in 2025

01:32:29.040 --> 01:32:32.940
and evidence of patient
and staff safety risks.

01:32:32.940 --> 01:32:35.550
Can the board explain
what action NHS England

01:32:35.550 --> 01:32:39.120
has taken to ensure legal
compliance across trusts,

01:32:39.120 --> 01:32:42.150
why previous requests to
review relevant policies

01:32:42.150 --> 01:32:45.780
were declined, and how it is
assuring itself that staff

01:32:45.780 --> 01:32:48.810
and patient safety is
not being compromised?

01:32:48.810 --> 01:32:49.643
- Thanks, Penny.

01:32:49.643 --> 01:32:51.570
So the couple of questions
we've had obviously cover

01:32:51.570 --> 01:32:53.370
kind of a linked but separate issues

01:32:53.370 --> 01:32:55.410
of single-sex facilities for staff

01:32:55.410 --> 01:32:57.900
and same-sex accommodation for patients.

01:32:57.900 --> 01:32:59.760
So in relation to staff facilities,

01:32:59.760 --> 01:33:03.780
which was the issue under consideration

01:33:03.780 --> 01:33:05.580
in the recent employment tribunal

01:33:05.580 --> 01:33:08.912
involving County Durham and
Darlington NHS Foundation Trust,

01:33:08.912 --> 01:33:12.150
NHS England does not issue
guidance for the system on this.

01:33:12.150 --> 01:33:14.880
It's for local organisations
to design their own policies

01:33:14.880 --> 01:33:18.060
and based on the relevant legislation.

01:33:18.060 --> 01:33:19.920
In terms of patient accommodation,

01:33:19.920 --> 01:33:22.140
NHS England does provide national guidance

01:33:22.140 --> 01:33:25.800
to support delivery of the
NHS Constitution Pledge.

01:33:25.800 --> 01:33:28.080
And as I said, at the last board meeting,

01:33:28.080 --> 01:33:29.970
NHS England has been reviewing this

01:33:29.970 --> 01:33:31.830
and all relevant policies and procedures

01:33:31.830 --> 01:33:35.220
to ensure they're aligned
with the Supreme Court ruling.

01:33:35.220 --> 01:33:36.480
However, it's important

01:33:36.480 --> 01:33:38.550
that the Equality and
Human Rights Commission,

01:33:38.550 --> 01:33:42.900
that's the EHRC updated code
of practise for services,

01:33:42.900 --> 01:33:44.820
public functions, and associations,

01:33:44.820 --> 01:33:47.220
which sets out the legal
framework for how services

01:33:47.220 --> 01:33:49.980
are delivered in line
with the Equality Act,

01:33:49.980 --> 01:33:52.590
is available before final decisions

01:33:52.590 --> 01:33:55.710
about national policies are taken.

01:33:55.710 --> 01:33:58.350
The updated code of practise
will be published shortly,

01:33:58.350 --> 01:34:00.360
subject to parliamentary approval

01:34:00.360 --> 01:34:02.940
and updating any guidance for the NHS

01:34:02.940 --> 01:34:04.890
will be available following that.

01:34:04.890 --> 01:34:07.170
We are continuing to
discuss with ministers

01:34:07.170 --> 01:34:08.640
in the Department of
Health and Social Care

01:34:08.640 --> 01:34:10.470
to ensure that NHS organisations

01:34:10.470 --> 01:34:12.630
have a clear national
steer on these issues

01:34:12.630 --> 01:34:14.130
as soon as possible.

01:34:14.130 --> 01:34:15.033
- Okay, thank you.

01:34:15.953 --> 01:34:18.300
And then the final question for Fiona,

01:34:18.300 --> 01:34:20.340
phase one of the NHS core list,

01:34:20.340 --> 01:34:22.860
which was launched in
February of this year,

01:34:22.860 --> 01:34:25.260
introduced nationally
agreed pricing frameworks

01:34:25.260 --> 01:34:30.260
to improve uptake and savings
through the NHS supply chain.

01:34:30.540 --> 01:34:32.910
Can the board set out how
learning from phase one

01:34:32.910 --> 01:34:34.830
will inform the prioritisation and design

01:34:34.830 --> 01:34:37.440
of future core list categories,

01:34:37.440 --> 01:34:40.470
including the role that ease
of implementation will play,

01:34:40.470 --> 01:34:43.680
how suppliers can propose
existing framework products

01:34:43.680 --> 01:34:46.920
with significant recurrent
savings for inclusion,

01:34:46.920 --> 01:34:48.780
and how NHS England assures

01:34:48.780 --> 01:34:50.820
that cost-driven product choices,

01:34:50.820 --> 01:34:54.840
including those used for
preoperative skin preparation

01:34:54.840 --> 01:34:57.990
continue to meet appropriate
regulatory licencing

01:34:57.990 --> 01:34:59.820
and patient safety standards.

01:34:59.820 --> 01:35:01.860
- Thank you, Penny. A few questions there.

01:35:01.860 --> 01:35:04.340
So I'll start with how
we learn from phase one

01:35:04.340 --> 01:35:05.910
of the core list.

01:35:05.910 --> 01:35:07.740
So feedback has already been gathered

01:35:07.740 --> 01:35:12.210
from key stakeholders,
both internal and external.

01:35:12.210 --> 01:35:13.980
And I think what's really important here

01:35:13.980 --> 01:35:16.890
is that we're currently
refreshing how this programme

01:35:16.890 --> 01:35:18.510
is going to work.

01:35:18.510 --> 01:35:20.790
That will ensure that this core list

01:35:20.790 --> 01:35:23.610
is underpinned by the robust processes

01:35:23.610 --> 01:35:26.820
and established timescales
that will enable the scaling

01:35:26.820 --> 01:35:28.950
and broadening to deliver greater value

01:35:28.950 --> 01:35:30.930
whilst managing the risk.

01:35:30.930 --> 01:35:32.700
And we're embedding those processes

01:35:32.700 --> 01:35:36.870
into the core activity, working
closely with supply chain.

01:35:36.870 --> 01:35:39.990
These learnings are
also going to be applied

01:35:39.990 --> 01:35:42.510
using this resilience and simplification,

01:35:42.510 --> 01:35:44.583
and we can share those more broadly.

01:35:45.750 --> 01:35:49.770
In terms of how we are going to ensure

01:35:49.770 --> 01:35:54.150
that this is used for future phrases,

01:35:54.150 --> 01:35:57.240
this will be worked up with
clinical colleagues, suppliers,

01:35:57.240 --> 01:36:01.020
and NHS partners, and in
the long-term ambition,

01:36:01.020 --> 01:36:04.440
it's really to look at how
many we can bring in safely,

01:36:04.440 --> 01:36:05.820
but I think most importantly,

01:36:05.820 --> 01:36:08.100
it's looking at product categories

01:36:08.100 --> 01:36:11.670
that will offer opportunities
to rationalise the range,

01:36:11.670 --> 01:36:13.680
remove the unwarranted variation

01:36:13.680 --> 01:36:15.450
we've heard a lot about today,

01:36:15.450 --> 01:36:19.683
but also to drive value
where there are low barriers.

01:36:20.670 --> 01:36:23.340
Lastly, I think in terms
of how these insights

01:36:23.340 --> 01:36:26.250
can really be signposted for suppliers,

01:36:26.250 --> 01:36:29.400
I think we have ongoing market engagement

01:36:29.400 --> 01:36:31.050
and we will continue to evolve

01:36:31.050 --> 01:36:34.470
to have one single entry
point for suppliers

01:36:34.470 --> 01:36:36.753
and customers as we move forwards.

01:36:37.830 --> 01:36:41.520
The last part of your question
was actually specifically

01:36:41.520 --> 01:36:46.040
around products that contain
active antiseptic ingredients

01:36:47.040 --> 01:36:50.340
that are intended for
preoperative skin preparation.

01:36:50.340 --> 01:36:53.670
These are defined as medical products,

01:36:53.670 --> 01:36:57.150
and therefore they must
hold the appropriate

01:36:57.150 --> 01:37:01.320
medicines and healthcare products
regulatory agency approval

01:37:01.320 --> 01:37:03.900
and authorization for that purpose.

01:37:03.900 --> 01:37:06.510
We work closely with the
Royal College of Surgeons

01:37:06.510 --> 01:37:10.800
and the MHRA joint statement
on how these products

01:37:10.800 --> 01:37:15.060
can be used clearly and
signposted and licenced

01:37:15.060 --> 01:37:17.160
for their intent.

01:37:17.160 --> 01:37:20.490
Therefore, NHS England
provides system level assurance

01:37:20.490 --> 01:37:23.100
through the NHS standard contract,

01:37:23.100 --> 01:37:24.870
National Infection Prevention Standards,

01:37:24.870 --> 01:37:27.510
Clinical Guidance and
Procurement Frameworks,

01:37:27.510 --> 01:37:32.010
all of which set clear
expectations that NHS trusts use

01:37:32.010 --> 01:37:34.530
clinical products that are
safe, clinically appropriate,

01:37:34.530 --> 01:37:37.413
and compliant with
regulatory requirements.

01:37:38.310 --> 01:37:41.610
- Great, that's quite an
interest useful prompt

01:37:41.610 --> 01:37:44.010
that we should probably
have an item on procurement

01:37:44.010 --> 01:37:47.340
and supply chains at a future meeting

01:37:47.340 --> 01:37:51.150
and also perhaps think
about how we look at that

01:37:51.150 --> 01:37:52.260
in terms of performance.

01:37:52.260 --> 01:37:54.360
Might be something to come back to.

01:37:54.360 --> 01:37:57.453
Okay, any other business from anyone?

01:37:58.860 --> 01:38:01.890
All right. Jolly good.
Thank you all very much.

01:38:01.890 --> 01:38:03.870
Well prepared. Look
forward to the next time.

01:38:03.870 --> 01:38:05.633
Thank you to members of
the public. Thank you.

